WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:12.490
- Thank you so much for joining us

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:14.270
for Afghanistan's Future:

00:00:14.270 --> 00:00:17.420
Development, The State and
The Humanitarian Challenge.

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:19.510
This is the 11th in the series

00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:21.140
The Yale Development Dialogues,

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:23.030
which are virtual panel discussions

00:00:23.030 --> 00:00:25.170
hosted by Yale's Economic Growth Center,

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:26.950
Jackson Institute for Global Affairs

00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:28.520
and History Department.

00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:29.550
I'm Catherine Cheney,

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:31.200
I'm a senior reporter at Devex,

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:34.150
focused on technology and
innovation in global development,

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:36.140
and I'm thrilled to moderate this series.

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:37.490
The Yale Development Dialogues,

00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:39.120
for those of you who aren't familiar,

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:41.410
brings together perspectives from history,

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:44.980
economics and policy to discuss
ways to address challenges

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:47.280
facing low and middle income countries.

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:48.910
And today we're joined by experts

00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:51.700
who have been at the forefront
of policy in Afghanistan

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:53.700
over the past two decades.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:56.010
So I have a number of
questions I'd like to ask them,

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:57.570
but for those of you joining us here

00:00:57.570 --> 00:00:59.240
live on the zoom webinar,

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:00.160
we really encourage you

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:02.530
to submit your questions
via the Q&amp;A function.

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:04.030
So keep those coming throughout

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:06.320
and I'll get to them as soon as I can.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:09.480
So transitioning to the
topic we're discussing today,

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:11.510
the American withdrawal from Afghanistan

00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:14.040
left the international
community uncertain,

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.610
not only in terms of the
future of the country

00:01:16.610 --> 00:01:17.850
and its people,

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:19.490
women and girls in particular,

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:22.110
but also the future of intervention.

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:24.290
So today we'll be asking questions like,

00:01:24.290 --> 00:01:26.340
how does the situation in Afghanistan

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:28.950
fit into a history of many failed,

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:31.730
and some productive interventions?

00:01:31.730 --> 00:01:32.750
What should the priorities

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:35.080
of the international
development community be

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:37.070
as they interact with the country now,

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:41.190
especially as the country faces
a real humanitarian crisis?

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:42.550
And what does the future hold

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:44.450
for the reconstruction of Afghanistan

00:01:44.450 --> 00:01:47.170
and its relationship with
the same foreign powers

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:49.350
who were previously invaders?

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:52.210
So joining us to discuss
these questions and more,

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:56.000
we're thrilled to be
joined by Dr. Orzala Nemat,

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:58.500
an Afghan scholar and think tank leader,

00:01:58.500 --> 00:01:59.930
who is Research Associate

00:01:59.930 --> 00:02:01.930
at the Department of Development Studies,

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:04.680
which is part of the School of
Oriental and African studies

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:06.270
in the UK.

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:07.610
Khalid Payenda,

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:09.730
who served as the last Minister of Finance

00:02:09.730 --> 00:02:11.960
in the Islamic Republic of Afghanistan,

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:14.730
leading an ambitious
portfolio of economic reforms

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:16.200
and anti-corruption initiatives

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:19.440
until his resignation in August, 2021.

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:21.150
And Rory Stewart, Senior Fellow

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:23.680
at Yale's Jackson Institute
for Global Affairs,

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:25.740
who served as the UK Secretary of State

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:27.370
for International Development.

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:30.350
His work focuses on
contemporary politics in crisis

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:32.400
and on international
development and intervention

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:34.690
in fragile and conflict-affected states.

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:37.173
And he's written
extensively on Afghanistan.

00:02:38.030 --> 00:02:39.974
Rory's also a co-organizer of this event,

00:02:39.974 --> 00:02:42.236
the Yale Development Dialogue series,

00:02:42.236 --> 00:02:44.950
and he spent a lot of time in Afghanistan,

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:48.440
from his solo walk across
the country in 2002

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:49.920
to his work starting and running

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:51.430
the Turquoise Mountain Foundation,

00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:55.050
to his role in government in the UK.

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:55.990
And Rory,

00:02:55.990 --> 00:02:56.940
I know you've been involved

00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:59.024
in a number of conversations
on Afghanistan recently.

00:02:59.024 --> 00:03:00.530
I've reviewed many of them

00:03:00.530 --> 00:03:02.100
in preparation for this discussion.

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:04.950
So I mentioned earlier the
Yale Development Dialogues

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:08.540
is unique in terms of the
interdisciplinary perspectives

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:09.630
it brings together.

00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:11.180
So I'd love to hear,

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:12.890
what are some of your thoughts and goals

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:14.300
for this particular conversation?

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:15.750
What can our audience expect?

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:21.640
- Thank you very, very much indeed.

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:23.350
And it's great to be here

00:03:23.350 --> 00:03:26.387
and a great privilege to be
with my two Afghan colleagues

00:03:26.387 --> 00:03:27.730
who I hope are going to be doing

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:29.470
the majority of the talking.

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:31.230
Let me just try to frame it slightly

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:33.051
from a Yale University point of view

00:03:33.051 --> 00:03:35.390
in the history of
international development.

00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:40.390
So Afghanistan is both a
very exceptional situation,

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:42.740
but also one that connects

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:46.490
to a much, much broader, longer history.

00:03:46.490 --> 00:03:49.301
So in terms of exception,

00:03:49.301 --> 00:03:52.810
people will think about it
as a military intervention,

00:03:52.810 --> 00:03:56.300
but it was also one of
the largest development,

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:58.930
international development
and humanitarian exercises,

00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:00.273
of the last 20 years.

00:04:01.250 --> 00:04:03.460
This was a country in which
the international community

00:04:03.460 --> 00:04:07.870
didn't only deploy nearly a
million military personnel

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:08.703
over the period,

00:04:08.703 --> 00:04:11.157
but hundreds of thousands of civilians

00:04:11.157 --> 00:04:13.410
who worked with Afghan partners

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:17.770
on almost every dimension
of economic development.

00:04:17.770 --> 00:04:19.870
On human rights, on civil society,

00:04:19.870 --> 00:04:22.350
on schooling, on health,

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:25.480
with absolutely unparalleled
levels of investment.

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:28.600
Now, as with any of these situations,

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:31.010
it is possible to criticize
a lot of that investment.

00:04:31.010 --> 00:04:33.590
Of course, much of the
money which was spent

00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:35.420
by the international
community in Afghanistan

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:38.630
ended up finding its way back to the West

00:04:38.630 --> 00:04:39.810
through strange contracts.

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:42.220
There was also of course,
a lot of corruption.

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:45.310
Nevertheless, there are
very, very few countries

00:04:45.310 --> 00:04:46.660
in the world over the last 20 years

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:49.750
where quite so much was invested.

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:52.450
And there was a very, very ambitious idea

00:04:52.450 --> 00:04:54.173
on remodeling a society.

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:58.390
So it gives us an opportunity to see what,

00:04:58.390 --> 00:05:00.650
with all the ideas of the West,

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:01.720
all the technology of the West

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:02.870
all the money of the West,

00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:06.210
what was and was not achieved
over a 20 year period

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:07.440
and how that compares to the way

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:08.780
we thought about international development

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:10.163
from the 1950s onwards.

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:13.680
And now of course, we find
ourselves in a new challenge,

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:16.815
which is the Taliban
government has taken over.

00:05:16.815 --> 00:05:20.700
People are trying to put pressure
on the Taliban government

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:23.970
and use development and
humanitarian assistance as leverage

00:05:23.970 --> 00:05:25.620
potentially over the Taliban government

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:29.340
at a time when there is an
extreme humanitarian crisis

00:05:29.340 --> 00:05:31.920
and Afghans are now facing starvation

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:34.040
in some parts of the country.

00:05:34.040 --> 00:05:36.400
So it then raises a
second series of questions

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:37.430
on the way in which we think

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:40.110
about international
development in the future.

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:40.970
So on that,

00:05:40.970 --> 00:05:44.019
I'm going to step aside for a bit

00:05:44.019 --> 00:05:47.610
and maybe hand back to my
Afghan colleagues to speak.

00:05:47.610 --> 00:05:48.443
Thank you.

00:05:49.810 --> 00:05:50.643
- Thank you, Rory.

00:05:50.643 --> 00:05:52.280
And I apologize for some background noise,

00:05:52.280 --> 00:05:53.930
unavoidable background
noise on my end here

00:05:53.930 --> 00:05:56.540
that will hopefully subside shortly.

00:05:56.540 --> 00:05:58.050
But one of the things that,

00:05:58.050 --> 00:06:00.820
Khalid mentioned in
preparation for this call

00:06:00.820 --> 00:06:03.300
was the importance of
beginning our conversation

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:04.720
with what was achieved.

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:07.360
While we're tasked with
talking about the future,

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:08.510
it is important to highlight

00:06:08.510 --> 00:06:10.570
what the 20 year intervention achieved.

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:13.283
So I wonder if you could
expand on that point.

00:06:14.450 --> 00:06:15.500
- Yeah, thank you very much.

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:18.515
It's a privilege to be here.

00:06:18.515 --> 00:06:21.210
Greetings to the esteemed panelists

00:06:21.210 --> 00:06:23.900
and also the moderator

00:06:23.900 --> 00:06:26.423
and the participants
who've joined us online.

00:06:27.380 --> 00:06:28.700
I wouldn't spend a lot of time,

00:06:28.700 --> 00:06:31.460
I think Rory beautifully
covered a part of it.

00:06:31.460 --> 00:06:33.400
You know, unprecedented amount of aid

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:34.450
flowing into our country,

00:06:34.450 --> 00:06:36.774
but it also achieved a lot.

00:06:36.774 --> 00:06:39.430
In the wake of the tragedy

00:06:39.430 --> 00:06:42.470
that we've seen over the last 90 days,

00:06:42.470 --> 00:06:47.470
it's difficult to get the
perspective of 20 years,

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:49.780
but a lot was achieved, you know.

00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:54.780
Going from a GDP per capita of below $200

00:06:55.440 --> 00:07:00.440
to tripling it over the
first decade and a half.

00:07:00.730 --> 00:07:03.600
Having 9 million children
go back to school, you know,

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:08.323
more than 30, 35% of them girls.

00:07:09.341 --> 00:07:13.300
Improvements on other
socioeconomic indicators,

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:16.870
you know, plummeting
maternal mortality numbers

00:07:16.870 --> 00:07:19.633
which were the worst in the world.

00:07:20.510 --> 00:07:22.944
Building institutions that were still,

00:07:22.944 --> 00:07:24.840
people complaining about,

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:28.000
but providing some service
deliveries, you know.

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:32.440
People making a nine, 10 hour trip

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:34.410
all the way to the border to Pakistan

00:07:34.410 --> 00:07:36.980
just to make a call to
a relative in the US.

00:07:36.980 --> 00:07:41.120
And the West could now have social media

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:43.340
and telecommunication, you know,

00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:45.733
and mobile at their fingertips.

00:07:47.726 --> 00:07:51.494
Afghanistan connected with
multiple flights a day

00:07:51.494 --> 00:07:53.443
flying out of Kabul.

00:07:54.365 --> 00:07:56.200
A lot of key infrastructure built

00:07:56.200 --> 00:07:58.900
that enabled integration
of the country better,

00:07:58.900 --> 00:08:03.200
but also enabled better
regional connectivity.

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:08.200
Had it been for better
relationships with the neighbors,

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:10.530
that key infrastructure

00:08:10.530 --> 00:08:12.100
could have done even much more.

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:13.283
So a lot was achieved.

00:08:15.770 --> 00:08:17.870
Obviously, with the
amount of money flowing

00:08:17.870 --> 00:08:21.360
with little accountability,

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:23.480
with systems that were to be built,

00:08:23.480 --> 00:08:24.880
obviously they were misused

00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:27.820
and were sometimes unfortunately,

00:08:27.820 --> 00:08:28.850
with good intentions,

00:08:28.850 --> 00:08:30.950
wastages that could have been avoided,

00:08:30.950 --> 00:08:32.240
but a lot was achieved.

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:36.040
I say this and I wanted
for us to start with,

00:08:36.040 --> 00:08:40.190
because in 2001, we faced
a similar situation.

00:08:40.190 --> 00:08:43.640
I think it's an incredibly,
incredibly difficult

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:45.040
people for the whole nation,

00:08:46.957 --> 00:08:50.229
but keeping a long-term
perspective helps, you know,

00:08:50.229 --> 00:08:55.080
having the faith that
things could be better.

00:08:55.080 --> 00:08:56.794
I'll stop here.

00:08:56.794 --> 00:08:57.627
- Absolutely.

00:08:57.627 --> 00:08:59.610
No, I appreciate you, you know,

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:01.920
starting us off with that
important perspective.

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:05.060
And zooming in on one example of progress,

00:09:05.060 --> 00:09:08.160
Orzala, I know you have over
two decades of experience

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:11.000
in development practice and
promoting women's rights

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:12.530
in war-affected societies.

00:09:12.530 --> 00:09:15.130
So I wonder if you can expand on the gains

00:09:15.130 --> 00:09:17.852
for Afghan women and girls
over the past two decades,

00:09:17.852 --> 00:09:20.773
and then we can talk
about where we are now.

00:09:22.956 --> 00:09:25.566
- Thank you very much Catherine.

00:09:25.566 --> 00:09:27.987
It's a great honor and pleasure

00:09:27.987 --> 00:09:31.829
to be part of this distinguished panel,

00:09:31.829 --> 00:09:35.210
and joining Rory and Khalid and yourself

00:09:35.210 --> 00:09:37.010
into this discussion.

00:09:37.010 --> 00:09:39.093
Very crucial and timely, I would say.

00:09:40.430 --> 00:09:45.023
With regards to gains of
the last twenty years,

00:09:45.023 --> 00:09:47.590
before even starting to talk about gains

00:09:47.590 --> 00:09:49.703
we have to sort of keep in mind

00:09:49.703 --> 00:09:54.703
that the gains come with
a lot of troubles as well.

00:09:54.902 --> 00:09:55.990
Generally speaking,

00:09:55.990 --> 00:09:58.000
and specifically with regards to women.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:01.390
So I just sort of divide the gains

00:10:01.390 --> 00:10:04.887
in terms of social,
political and economic.

00:10:04.887 --> 00:10:07.930
And very briefly, run through
all of it just for us,

00:10:07.930 --> 00:10:09.270
for our sort of memories,

00:10:09.270 --> 00:10:11.720
to remind ourselves of
what has been the changes.

00:10:13.102 --> 00:10:14.520
In the social field,

00:10:14.520 --> 00:10:19.520
what we see have been
achieved is a massive and...

00:10:21.261 --> 00:10:23.880
excuse me, there was an annoying sound,

00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:25.800
I hope you now don't hear it.

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:28.271
My notebook was getting somewhere.

00:10:28.271 --> 00:10:30.250
- [Catherine] Our fault.

00:10:30.250 --> 00:10:31.803
- I hope now it's clearer.

00:10:33.070 --> 00:10:34.890
So in terms of social gains,

00:10:34.890 --> 00:10:39.130
what I'd like to highlight
is this change in people,

00:10:39.130 --> 00:10:41.680
particularly, generally in Afghanistan,

00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:42.870
in rural Afghanistan

00:10:42.870 --> 00:10:46.710
in terms of people's behaviors
towards women's rights,

00:10:46.710 --> 00:10:48.983
women's position in society.

00:10:49.970 --> 00:10:53.871
There has been some unprecedented
level of achievements

00:10:53.871 --> 00:10:58.140
in the rural and community
development side of things.

00:10:58.140 --> 00:11:00.450
In some communities and in some areas

00:11:00.450 --> 00:11:03.080
that I have gone because
of my own research

00:11:03.080 --> 00:11:05.640
on local power relations
and local governance,

00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:07.410
I find out that for the first time

00:11:07.410 --> 00:11:09.780
in the history of these areas,

00:11:09.780 --> 00:11:12.930
the state and the donors
have reached there

00:11:12.930 --> 00:11:16.480
not to extract, but to deliver.

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:18.980
So this is something that we cannot deny.

00:11:18.980 --> 00:11:20.560
Nobody, including the Taliban,

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:24.323
cannot deny the impact of
the community development.

00:11:25.170 --> 00:11:29.210
And the role of women in the
community development programs.

00:11:29.210 --> 00:11:31.380
The conditionality that was set up

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:33.670
has been a game changer.

00:11:33.670 --> 00:11:35.550
Now, having said that,

00:11:35.550 --> 00:11:38.840
in terms of social behavioral
change has happened.

00:11:38.840 --> 00:11:41.650
We see a thirst for education.

00:11:41.650 --> 00:11:44.330
We see that thirst for
education across the country,

00:11:44.330 --> 00:11:47.400
across generations, across gender.

00:11:47.400 --> 00:11:50.190
There is a pin pad campaign by volunteers

00:11:50.190 --> 00:11:51.550
from the Southern Afghanistan,

00:11:51.550 --> 00:11:55.300
running around with the
men that in appearance

00:11:55.300 --> 00:11:57.659
to all internationals look like a Taliban,

00:11:57.659 --> 00:11:58.660
no different from a Taliban.

00:11:58.660 --> 00:11:59.979
Long beard, big turban,

00:11:59.979 --> 00:12:02.780
having a paper,

00:12:02.780 --> 00:12:04.677
a placard in their hand that says

00:12:04.677 --> 00:12:06.320
open the schools for girls.

00:12:06.320 --> 00:12:08.840
So that's something that
has a message for everyone,

00:12:08.840 --> 00:12:12.600
particularly for those who
are ruling the country now.

00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:13.970
The political games,

00:12:13.970 --> 00:12:16.790
although in many aspects quite symbolic,

00:12:16.790 --> 00:12:18.770
but has been also important.

00:12:18.770 --> 00:12:21.857
We've had in the constitution 25% quotas,

00:12:21.857 --> 00:12:26.620
but a lot of women made
it beyond that quota

00:12:26.620 --> 00:12:31.310
in the provincial council
and parliamentary elections.

00:12:31.310 --> 00:12:33.890
So the symbolic part aside,

00:12:33.890 --> 00:12:35.220
and the political reason,

00:12:35.220 --> 00:12:37.273
some of the behaviors of the politicians

00:12:37.273 --> 00:12:41.180
using instrumentally
women position was there,

00:12:41.180 --> 00:12:43.980
but women themselves have created

00:12:43.980 --> 00:12:48.660
an identity for themselves
to be very particular.

00:12:48.660 --> 00:12:50.760
Not necessarily representing themselves

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:53.610
as the representatives
of the Western values

00:12:53.610 --> 00:12:55.940
or Eastern values or anything.

00:12:55.940 --> 00:12:57.320
Very pure, very traditional,

00:12:57.320 --> 00:12:58.440
but at the same time

00:12:58.440 --> 00:13:00.510
very aware and conscious of their rights.

00:13:00.510 --> 00:13:02.240
I think these are critical.

00:13:02.240 --> 00:13:03.410
And just very quickly,

00:13:03.410 --> 00:13:05.480
I know I've gone beyond my two minutes,

00:13:05.480 --> 00:13:06.450
but just very quickly,

00:13:06.450 --> 00:13:10.398
in terms of economic gains as well,

00:13:10.398 --> 00:13:13.670
we have to remember 70%
of Afghan population

00:13:13.670 --> 00:13:14.820
lives in rural areas,

00:13:14.820 --> 00:13:17.400
but the economic gains has
also been quite significant

00:13:17.400 --> 00:13:18.430
in the rural context

00:13:18.430 --> 00:13:21.486
where women were able to access markets,

00:13:21.486 --> 00:13:24.313
sell their products to the
level possible, obviously,

00:13:24.313 --> 00:13:27.630
and I'm just worried what
will happen to it now.

00:13:27.630 --> 00:13:28.840
Women were able to make,

00:13:28.840 --> 00:13:30.440
to be involved in businesses,

00:13:30.440 --> 00:13:32.450
small, medium, and larger scales,

00:13:32.450 --> 00:13:34.115
more in the small and medium range.

00:13:34.115 --> 00:13:36.076
Less in larger, because of course,

00:13:36.076 --> 00:13:37.958
the men are trying to sort of,

00:13:37.958 --> 00:13:40.210
men earn more over them.

00:13:40.210 --> 00:13:44.750
So overall, in all these
different sort of areas

00:13:44.750 --> 00:13:47.133
there has been quite significant gains

00:13:47.133 --> 00:13:48.530
that we cannot deny it.

00:13:48.530 --> 00:13:49.940
And that's something

00:13:49.940 --> 00:13:52.051
that with the crisis that is happening now

00:13:52.051 --> 00:13:53.610
may be affected.

00:13:53.610 --> 00:13:55.450
Maybe we will see the different levels

00:13:55.450 --> 00:13:56.800
and maybe we'll see some changes,

00:13:56.800 --> 00:13:58.980
but there will not be a kind of a dragging

00:13:58.980 --> 00:14:00.690
to the zero point again.

00:14:00.690 --> 00:14:02.210
That's my sort of,

00:14:02.210 --> 00:14:06.330
bit of an optimism in terms
of moving way forward.

00:14:06.330 --> 00:14:07.163
Thanks.
- Thank you.

00:14:07.163 --> 00:14:08.480
Rory, please jump in.

00:14:08.480 --> 00:14:09.870
- A couple of quick things.

00:14:09.870 --> 00:14:11.620
I mean, I think the
first thing to understand

00:14:11.620 --> 00:14:14.910
is that the story in Afghanistan is uneven

00:14:14.910 --> 00:14:16.180
depending upon which part

00:14:16.180 --> 00:14:18.147
of the country you're talking about.

00:14:18.147 --> 00:14:19.700
So in many ways there was huge progress,

00:14:19.700 --> 00:14:21.083
not just in urban areas.

00:14:21.083 --> 00:14:23.420
Obviously in places that the cities,

00:14:23.420 --> 00:14:27.430
but also in places like the
Bamiyan Valley, Hazarajat,

00:14:27.430 --> 00:14:30.370
extraordinary transformation
over 20 years.

00:14:30.370 --> 00:14:32.780
Other areas of the country, for example,

00:14:32.780 --> 00:14:35.040
the Sangin district in Helmand.

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:36.770
Very, very difficult.

00:14:36.770 --> 00:14:40.080
And in some ways the lives
of women in those communities

00:14:40.080 --> 00:14:41.910
didn't change as much
as we would have liked

00:14:41.910 --> 00:14:42.990
over the period.

00:14:42.990 --> 00:14:44.110
Partly because they were in the middle

00:14:44.110 --> 00:14:45.020
of a horrible conflict.

00:14:45.020 --> 00:14:46.800
Drug wars, warlords,

00:14:46.800 --> 00:14:48.913
and ultimately a Taliban insurgency.

00:14:50.750 --> 00:14:53.647
On the second point
that Dr. Orzala has made

00:14:53.647 --> 00:14:55.580
about small businesses,

00:14:55.580 --> 00:14:57.232
this is absolutely vital.

00:14:57.232 --> 00:14:58.630
And there's actually a great deal

00:14:58.630 --> 00:15:01.566
the international
community can do to help.

00:15:01.566 --> 00:15:04.730
At the moment there are
nearly a million Afghan women

00:15:04.730 --> 00:15:07.293
working in the carpet industry,

00:15:07.293 --> 00:15:11.440
and that industry has amazing potential.

00:15:11.440 --> 00:15:12.970
You don't need to think about it,

00:15:12.970 --> 00:15:14.300
as it might've been in the past,

00:15:14.300 --> 00:15:15.830
as a place which was dominated

00:15:15.830 --> 00:15:17.593
by minimum wages and child labor.

00:15:18.540 --> 00:15:19.700
We were beginning,

00:15:19.700 --> 00:15:20.740
in the last couple of years,

00:15:20.740 --> 00:15:24.170
to secure some very high value
contracts for Afghan carpets,

00:15:24.170 --> 00:15:28.180
selling to big American and
European buyers and suppliers,

00:15:28.180 --> 00:15:30.420
setting up good weaving workshops

00:15:30.420 --> 00:15:33.450
with good conditions for
children and creches.

00:15:33.450 --> 00:15:35.260
And actually, as Turkey and India

00:15:35.260 --> 00:15:37.190
moves out of the handmade carpet industry,

00:15:37.190 --> 00:15:41.910
that could be a huge
opportunity for Afghans

00:15:41.910 --> 00:15:43.580
to generate small and
medium size enterprises.

00:15:43.580 --> 00:15:46.940
The problem is that the
international community now

00:15:46.940 --> 00:15:48.920
is not putting any more investment.

00:15:48.920 --> 00:15:50.570
USAID said it's not going to support

00:15:50.570 --> 00:15:52.440
the export industry anymore.

00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:53.273
Everybody's saying

00:15:53.273 --> 00:15:55.490
we're just going to think
about humanitarian support,

00:15:55.490 --> 00:15:58.060
and this is classified
as development support.

00:15:58.060 --> 00:15:58.893
And this will mean,

00:15:58.893 --> 00:16:00.470
very unnecessarily and cruelly,

00:16:00.470 --> 00:16:02.660
that these great export opportunities,

00:16:02.660 --> 00:16:04.850
these contracts that women
have managed to secure

00:16:04.850 --> 00:16:06.802
with huge difficulty,

00:16:06.802 --> 00:16:08.260
are now under threat

00:16:08.260 --> 00:16:10.640
from the international community itself,

00:16:10.640 --> 00:16:13.280
often for quite unconvincing reasons

00:16:13.280 --> 00:16:16.020
to do with unrealistic views

00:16:16.020 --> 00:16:17.870
of how they can leverage the Taliban.

00:16:19.662 --> 00:16:20.495
- I think that's a great example.

00:16:20.495 --> 00:16:22.650
When it comes to questions,

00:16:22.650 --> 00:16:23.483
we'll get to around to the role

00:16:23.483 --> 00:16:26.800
of the international development
community in Afghanistan.

00:16:26.800 --> 00:16:30.500
Some of the decisions
being made are hampering

00:16:30.500 --> 00:16:31.861
some potential progress here,

00:16:31.861 --> 00:16:33.500
for example, in the carpet industry,

00:16:33.500 --> 00:16:34.873
really interesting.

00:16:34.873 --> 00:16:39.590
I wanted to talk about the withdrawal

00:16:39.590 --> 00:16:41.240
and certainly make sure we talk about that

00:16:41.240 --> 00:16:43.530
before we look at the current state

00:16:43.530 --> 00:16:45.750
of the country and the future.

00:16:45.750 --> 00:16:47.556
So you know, as I mentioned earlier,

00:16:47.556 --> 00:16:50.290
the dramatic collapse of the military

00:16:50.290 --> 00:16:51.760
and takeover by the Taliban

00:16:51.760 --> 00:16:53.870
really left the international
community uncertain

00:16:53.870 --> 00:16:55.710
about not just the future of the country,

00:16:55.710 --> 00:16:57.360
but the future of intervention.

00:16:57.360 --> 00:16:58.193
And Rory,

00:16:58.193 --> 00:17:00.691
I know as you and I discussed
the framing of this event,

00:17:00.691 --> 00:17:04.680
we wanted to talk not just about
Afghanistan and its future,

00:17:04.680 --> 00:17:06.020
which is our main task,

00:17:06.020 --> 00:17:08.510
but also how this fits into
a history of interventions.

00:17:08.510 --> 00:17:10.300
And that speaks to the spirit

00:17:10.300 --> 00:17:12.110
of the Yale Development
Dialogues, weaving in history.

00:17:12.110 --> 00:17:14.490
So can you expand on that?

00:17:14.490 --> 00:17:16.930
Just kind of putting this
into larger historical context

00:17:16.930 --> 00:17:18.340
and then I'd love to see
if our other panelists

00:17:18.340 --> 00:17:19.980
have additional thoughts.

00:17:19.980 --> 00:17:20.813
- Yeah,

00:17:20.813 --> 00:17:25.732
so essentially Afghanistan
was the last big intervention

00:17:25.732 --> 00:17:28.790
in a 20 year period of intervention,

00:17:28.790 --> 00:17:31.250
which started with Bosnia and Kosovo,

00:17:31.250 --> 00:17:32.980
which actually gave
quite a lot of confidence

00:17:32.980 --> 00:17:33.990
to the international community

00:17:33.990 --> 00:17:35.258
because in Bosnia and Kosovo,

00:17:35.258 --> 00:17:37.070
essentially there was an intervention,

00:17:37.070 --> 00:17:39.150
it was largely peaceful, a war ended.

00:17:39.150 --> 00:17:41.900
There was no insurgency
and progress was made.

00:17:41.900 --> 00:17:42.840
And that in a sense

00:17:42.840 --> 00:17:46.940
drove some of the investment
into Iraq and Afghanistan,

00:17:46.940 --> 00:17:49.390
which of course were the
two later interventions.

00:17:50.330 --> 00:17:51.940
In the case of Afghanistan itself

00:17:51.940 --> 00:17:53.280
there were three distinct phases.

00:17:53.280 --> 00:17:55.560
There was an initial phase
of quite a light footprint,

00:17:55.560 --> 00:17:57.523
very few troops not doing very much.

00:17:58.400 --> 00:17:59.740
Then the international community

00:17:59.740 --> 00:18:02.014
got dragged into doing
more and more and more

00:18:02.014 --> 00:18:03.500
because they felt that the situation

00:18:03.500 --> 00:18:07.360
in 2001, 2005 was not satisfactory.

00:18:07.360 --> 00:18:09.750
And then they went back to
a light footprint again.

00:18:09.750 --> 00:18:12.400
The issue however,

00:18:12.400 --> 00:18:15.400
is that the lesson that will
be taken from Afghanistan

00:18:15.400 --> 00:18:17.430
is not actually the points

00:18:17.430 --> 00:18:20.350
that Khalid or Dr. Orzala have made

00:18:20.350 --> 00:18:22.330
about how much was achieved.

00:18:22.330 --> 00:18:25.760
In the United States and
in Europe, unfortunately,

00:18:25.760 --> 00:18:28.649
people are convincing themselves
that nothing was achieved,

00:18:28.649 --> 00:18:30.263
that the whole thing was a mess.

00:18:31.837 --> 00:18:34.210
Which is completely not true.

00:18:34.210 --> 00:18:36.250
I mean, for any of us who saw Afghanistan

00:18:36.250 --> 00:18:37.420
at the end of the Taliban period,

00:18:37.420 --> 00:18:42.420
the difference between then
and 2021 was unbelievable.

00:18:42.530 --> 00:18:44.940
I mean, Kabul was a ghost
town at the end of 2001.

00:18:44.940 --> 00:18:47.180
You know, when I walked
from Herat to Kabul

00:18:47.180 --> 00:18:50.153
there was no electricity
for 350 miles in 2001.

00:18:50.153 --> 00:18:52.903
I mean, it's a totally different country.

00:18:54.840 --> 00:18:59.840
But we need to start
rebuilding some confidence

00:19:01.450 --> 00:19:02.530
amongst the international community

00:19:02.530 --> 00:19:04.203
that something can be achieved.

00:19:05.100 --> 00:19:07.330
And it's about getting out
of a black and white mindset.

00:19:07.330 --> 00:19:08.163
Of course,

00:19:08.163 --> 00:19:09.610
we've proved that you can't build

00:19:09.610 --> 00:19:11.113
a perfect nation under fire,

00:19:12.200 --> 00:19:13.710
but that doesn't mean that
you can't do anything.

00:19:13.710 --> 00:19:15.230
That doesn't mean that the whole project

00:19:15.230 --> 00:19:17.030
of international development
is a waste of time

00:19:17.030 --> 00:19:18.710
or there's nothing that you can do.

00:19:18.710 --> 00:19:21.490
Of course there's
corruption, there's waste,

00:19:21.490 --> 00:19:26.260
but I think Khalid really
put his finger on it

00:19:26.260 --> 00:19:27.610
in putting that in context.

00:19:27.610 --> 00:19:29.830
Explaining yes, those things happen.

00:19:29.830 --> 00:19:31.100
They don't just happen in Afghanistan.

00:19:31.100 --> 00:19:32.432
They happen in Sub-Saharan Africa.

00:19:32.432 --> 00:19:34.220
They happen in Myanmar.

00:19:34.220 --> 00:19:35.520
They happen in many places

00:19:36.440 --> 00:19:37.853
and things can go wrong.

00:19:38.760 --> 00:19:40.350
But when things go wrong,

00:19:40.350 --> 00:19:42.190
when the Taliban takes over,

00:19:42.190 --> 00:19:44.183
don't conclude from that,

00:19:44.183 --> 00:19:48.020
that that is because all Afghans
want a Taliban government.

00:19:48.020 --> 00:19:50.240
That's the other mistake
that people are making.

00:19:50.240 --> 00:19:52.180
The Taliban didn't come into these towns

00:19:52.180 --> 00:19:53.600
with everybody waving flags

00:19:53.600 --> 00:19:55.820
saying welcome the Taliban, right?

00:19:55.820 --> 00:19:57.930
This is an armed jihadist group.

00:19:57.930 --> 00:19:59.273
They were not elected.

00:20:00.460 --> 00:20:02.700
And Afghan's been fighting for 20 years

00:20:02.700 --> 00:20:07.460
and ultimately people
chose not to commit suicide

00:20:07.460 --> 00:20:08.550
trying to fight the Taliban,

00:20:08.550 --> 00:20:12.220
but that doesn't mean that
they've chosen the Taliban.

00:20:12.220 --> 00:20:14.400
And that doesn't mean
that Afghans as a whole

00:20:14.400 --> 00:20:17.160
think that the last 20
years was a waste of time

00:20:17.160 --> 00:20:20.100
and they prefer the situation
they have at the moment.

00:20:20.100 --> 00:20:20.933
- Absolutely.

00:20:20.933 --> 00:20:23.163
Khalid or Orzala, you
wanna jump in on that?

00:20:28.225 --> 00:20:32.600
- Yeah. I second everything Rory said,

00:20:32.600 --> 00:20:34.840
including his last intervention

00:20:34.840 --> 00:20:39.000
on the differences in how

00:20:39.000 --> 00:20:41.200
intervention was done within provinces.

00:20:41.200 --> 00:20:44.890
It also had to do a lot with
winning hearts and minds.

00:20:44.890 --> 00:20:48.130
Somehow military did not do
what militaries do, you know.

00:20:48.130 --> 00:20:51.790
They went and created parallel structures,

00:20:51.790 --> 00:20:55.300
dwarfing the other institutions

00:20:55.300 --> 00:20:58.170
that were supposed to
do aid work, you know.

00:20:58.170 --> 00:21:01.050
The US military spending in some years,

00:21:01.050 --> 00:21:03.350
a hundred times more on development,

00:21:03.350 --> 00:21:06.160
then USA is going to skew

00:21:06.160 --> 00:21:10.780
the whole development agenda.

00:21:10.780 --> 00:21:12.656
And unfortunately you know,

00:21:12.656 --> 00:21:16.340
the size of the national
budget was so small

00:21:16.340 --> 00:21:18.570
and so much was spent outside the budget

00:21:18.570 --> 00:21:21.561
that the government had
a real problem, you know,

00:21:21.561 --> 00:21:26.561
showing and increasing its legitimacy.

00:21:26.750 --> 00:21:30.940
And so these things really did have a role

00:21:30.940 --> 00:21:33.640
in how things played.

00:21:33.640 --> 00:21:35.365
And unfortunately, you know,

00:21:35.365 --> 00:21:37.860
when you look at some of the lessons

00:21:37.860 --> 00:21:39.390
that come out of Afghanistan.

00:21:39.390 --> 00:21:40.560
it's not the first time, you know.

00:21:40.560 --> 00:21:42.750
If you look at aid
effectiveness principles

00:21:42.750 --> 00:21:44.337
of what you should do and don't,

00:21:44.337 --> 00:21:47.038
and how you expand and
what channels you use

00:21:47.038 --> 00:21:50.170
and how the government could be a partner,

00:21:50.170 --> 00:21:54.070
unfortunately not much has been learned.

00:21:54.070 --> 00:21:57.590
There is a lot of lessons
that are reiterated

00:21:57.590 --> 00:21:59.593
from Afghanistan that should be learned,

00:21:59.593 --> 00:22:02.550
but then I'm not very optimistic

00:22:02.550 --> 00:22:05.200
about the behavior of these
international institutions

00:22:05.200 --> 00:22:08.370
and learning those those
lessons from Afghanistan

00:22:09.670 --> 00:22:11.960
for hopefully, you know,

00:22:11.960 --> 00:22:14.800
improved intervention in Afghanistan

00:22:14.800 --> 00:22:19.646
from now on or somewhere else.

00:22:19.646 --> 00:22:20.479
- Orzala,

00:22:20.479 --> 00:22:22.700
anything to add in terms of lessons

00:22:22.700 --> 00:22:27.313
that should have been
learned to inform, you know.

00:22:28.470 --> 00:22:29.303
- Yes.

00:22:29.303 --> 00:22:31.318
- Had lessons from history
been considered, perhaps,

00:22:31.318 --> 00:22:33.450
things would have unfolded differently

00:22:33.450 --> 00:22:36.760
and/or lessons that we
should be taking now from,

00:22:36.760 --> 00:22:39.280
you know, the crisis that has unfolded

00:22:39.280 --> 00:22:41.113
to inform work, moving forward.

00:22:42.130 --> 00:22:44.380
- Thank you Catherine,

00:22:44.380 --> 00:22:46.660
adding to things that have been mentioned,

00:22:46.660 --> 00:22:48.143
I think Afghanistan,

00:22:49.290 --> 00:22:52.870
in terms of many fields,

00:22:52.870 --> 00:22:55.060
whether international relations

00:22:55.060 --> 00:22:59.000
or even many other economics, whatever,

00:22:59.000 --> 00:23:01.263
particularly including I would say,

00:23:01.263 --> 00:23:03.390
development studies field,

00:23:03.390 --> 00:23:08.103
I think it's a case study
in all different ways.

00:23:08.103 --> 00:23:10.770
So in that sense,

00:23:10.770 --> 00:23:13.870
my hope and desire is
to see all, you know,

00:23:13.870 --> 00:23:17.050
educational institutions
globally across the world

00:23:17.050 --> 00:23:18.280
in the developing world,

00:23:18.280 --> 00:23:20.180
but also in the developed world,

00:23:20.180 --> 00:23:22.087
they have to pick Afghanistan

00:23:22.087 --> 00:23:27.087
and study each and every project
and program interventions.

00:23:27.370 --> 00:23:31.440
And there is a tremendous
amount of lessons to be learned

00:23:31.440 --> 00:23:32.900
in all of it.

00:23:32.900 --> 00:23:35.240
I go about some general lessons learned

00:23:35.240 --> 00:23:36.570
because I'm sure you know,

00:23:36.570 --> 00:23:40.022
Yale, and the politics department
and everything can get it

00:23:40.022 --> 00:23:41.580
sort of to higher up.

00:23:41.580 --> 00:23:43.250
I think one of the biggest lessons

00:23:43.250 --> 00:23:46.660
that the intervening forces need to learn

00:23:46.660 --> 00:23:49.633
is to stop investing on individuals.

00:23:50.810 --> 00:23:53.016
We started with making Karzai

00:23:53.016 --> 00:23:56.710
the most well-dressed man of the year.

00:23:56.710 --> 00:24:00.350
And we ended up with
calling him, I don't know,

00:24:00.350 --> 00:24:01.585
all kinds of,

00:24:01.585 --> 00:24:03.800
a man with all kinds of illnesses

00:24:03.800 --> 00:24:05.600
and schizophrenic and whatever.

00:24:05.600 --> 00:24:07.210
We started, Ashraf Ghani,

00:24:07.210 --> 00:24:10.250
with calling him the
second thoughtful man.

00:24:10.250 --> 00:24:13.270
I don't know how to translate
that back into English,

00:24:13.270 --> 00:24:14.970
but Theorist in Chief,

00:24:14.970 --> 00:24:17.430
I looked up in Google.

00:24:17.430 --> 00:24:18.983
You know, he was...

00:24:18.983 --> 00:24:21.450
- The second cleverest
man in the world, we said.

00:24:21.450 --> 00:24:22.656
- Something like that, right?

00:24:22.656 --> 00:24:24.090
So we call him,

00:24:24.090 --> 00:24:25.883
the New York,

00:24:27.280 --> 00:24:29.340
New Yorker actually,

00:24:29.340 --> 00:24:31.810
call him the Theorist in Chief

00:24:32.710 --> 00:24:34.940
and then, look at what happened, right?

00:24:34.940 --> 00:24:38.210
So stop investing on individuals

00:24:38.210 --> 00:24:39.980
when you intervene in a country.

00:24:39.980 --> 00:24:43.200
It will end up in a disaster, as it did.

00:24:43.200 --> 00:24:44.660
The other biggest lesson,

00:24:44.660 --> 00:24:46.430
I think to go very broadly,

00:24:46.430 --> 00:24:49.230
is the issue of donor dependency.

00:24:49.230 --> 00:24:52.640
The starvation that Rory
was mentioning before,

00:24:52.640 --> 00:24:56.020
the situation, the catastrophe
that we are facing now,

00:24:56.020 --> 00:24:57.570
it's quite shocking in fact,

00:24:57.570 --> 00:24:58.990
if you think about it.

00:24:58.990 --> 00:25:01.010
One in two people don't know

00:25:01.010 --> 00:25:03.060
where the next food is coming from.

00:25:03.060 --> 00:25:05.940
And I literally witnessed
that on a daily basis

00:25:05.940 --> 00:25:10.600
dealing with my own friends
and relatives and colleagues.

00:25:10.600 --> 00:25:13.265
This is the depth of the crisis.

00:25:13.265 --> 00:25:15.820
You cannot put this all on a government

00:25:15.820 --> 00:25:18.260
or a failed government or one party.

00:25:18.260 --> 00:25:21.020
I think from United Nations to World Bank

00:25:21.020 --> 00:25:23.760
to all the donor conferences
and fancy, you know,

00:25:23.760 --> 00:25:24.660
reports and everything,

00:25:24.660 --> 00:25:26.470
everyone should question themselves,

00:25:26.470 --> 00:25:29.760
why we led everything to
this level of dependency.

00:25:29.760 --> 00:25:32.320
And last but not least,

00:25:32.320 --> 00:25:35.060
one other part that I would like to go on

00:25:35.060 --> 00:25:38.703
is aggressive means always fail.

00:25:39.640 --> 00:25:43.180
The whole of war on terror was a failure,

00:25:43.180 --> 00:25:44.523
we have to note that.

00:25:45.370 --> 00:25:49.660
The whole mission was
to go and "smoke out",

00:25:49.660 --> 00:25:52.830
quote, unquote, the terrorists.

00:25:52.830 --> 00:25:54.093
Guess what?

00:25:54.093 --> 00:25:55.730
Those you call terrorists

00:25:55.730 --> 00:25:59.410
are handed over back the whole country on.

00:25:59.410 --> 00:26:01.760
We don't go into blaming game here,

00:26:01.760 --> 00:26:03.500
but aggressive means,

00:26:03.500 --> 00:26:05.595
Guantanamo Bay, drone attacks,

00:26:05.595 --> 00:26:07.620
night raids, didn't work.

00:26:07.620 --> 00:26:09.160
And that's what we see.

00:26:09.160 --> 00:26:11.820
The Taliban are more aggressive
today because of those,

00:26:11.820 --> 00:26:13.880
they are reacting against the actions

00:26:13.880 --> 00:26:15.610
that were taken against them.

00:26:15.610 --> 00:26:17.537
So I think these are key lessons

00:26:17.537 --> 00:26:21.040
that we need to sort of
look into stopping here.

00:26:21.040 --> 00:26:22.100
- Rory, go ahead.

00:26:22.100 --> 00:26:24.930
- One thing I would add is
the international community

00:26:24.930 --> 00:26:27.870
being modest and humble with itself

00:26:27.870 --> 00:26:29.570
and with others about what it can achieve.

00:26:29.570 --> 00:26:31.650
One of the problems is
that when you're spending,

00:26:31.650 --> 00:26:33.190
and when I was Secretary of State

00:26:33.190 --> 00:26:34.030
for International Development

00:26:34.030 --> 00:26:34.960
I felt this pressure,

00:26:34.960 --> 00:26:37.180
you're spending billions of dollars

00:26:37.180 --> 00:26:38.160
in somebody else's country.

00:26:38.160 --> 00:26:41.000
So there's a lot of
pressure to tell the voters

00:26:41.000 --> 00:26:42.400
in Europe or the United States

00:26:42.400 --> 00:26:44.950
that we're going to transform everything.

00:26:44.950 --> 00:26:48.070
And you also are always
showing off towards Afghans,

00:26:48.070 --> 00:26:49.350
telling them, "It's all going to be great,

00:26:49.350 --> 00:26:50.210
we know what we're doing.

00:26:50.210 --> 00:26:51.920
We have best practice, capacity building,

00:26:51.920 --> 00:26:53.820
it's all going to be amazing."

00:26:53.820 --> 00:26:55.790
It would be very helpful to,

00:26:55.790 --> 00:26:56.800
from the beginning,

00:26:56.800 --> 00:26:58.833
set more realistic expectations.

00:26:59.875 --> 00:27:03.420
The truth is the history of
international development

00:27:03.420 --> 00:27:06.500
in a country with a lot of
challenges like Afghanistan,

00:27:06.500 --> 00:27:08.920
and as Khalid has pointed out,

00:27:08.920 --> 00:27:12.253
this was one of the poorest
countries in the world in 2001.

00:27:13.440 --> 00:27:16.920
We had a situation where
many villages in 2001,

00:27:16.920 --> 00:27:19.030
there was only maybe one or
two people in the whole village

00:27:19.030 --> 00:27:21.850
who could read or write, right?

00:27:21.850 --> 00:27:23.910
And it's almost impossible to know

00:27:23.910 --> 00:27:26.520
what the infant mortality rate was

00:27:26.520 --> 00:27:28.490
because there was no proper registration

00:27:28.490 --> 00:27:29.960
of births and deaths under the Taliban,

00:27:29.960 --> 00:27:32.300
but we know it was terrible, right?

00:27:32.300 --> 00:27:34.540
So if you're starting from that level

00:27:34.540 --> 00:27:36.687
to be very realistic with Afghans

00:27:36.687 --> 00:27:37.940
and with your own voters

00:27:37.940 --> 00:27:39.290
about what you can achieve.

00:27:40.500 --> 00:27:43.110
So instead of saying,
we're going to create,

00:27:43.110 --> 00:27:45.240
and this is something we all did,

00:27:45.240 --> 00:27:46.130
British politicians did it,

00:27:46.130 --> 00:27:47.830
but Afghan politicians did it too.

00:27:47.830 --> 00:27:48.740
You know, Ashraf Ghani,

00:27:48.740 --> 00:27:51.670
I remember when I saw him
at the beginning of 2002

00:27:51.670 --> 00:27:53.640
saying we're gonna create
a gender-sensitive,

00:27:53.640 --> 00:27:55.000
multi-ethnic, centralized state

00:27:55.000 --> 00:27:56.240
based on democracy, human rights,

00:27:56.240 --> 00:27:57.223
rule of law, right?

00:27:58.290 --> 00:28:00.500
It's not very helpful.

00:28:00.500 --> 00:28:02.640
Of course, maybe that's the long-term aim

00:28:02.640 --> 00:28:07.120
but begin by saying our
aim is over 10 or 20 years

00:28:07.120 --> 00:28:10.330
to make Afghanistan more prosperous,

00:28:10.330 --> 00:28:12.300
improve the schools, improve the clinics.

00:28:12.300 --> 00:28:13.220
It's gonna be slow.

00:28:13.220 --> 00:28:14.710
It's gonna take a lot of time.

00:28:14.710 --> 00:28:17.010
And by building that spirit,

00:28:17.010 --> 00:28:18.719
you're not going to get so
many frustrated Afghans,

00:28:18.719 --> 00:28:20.970
you're not gonna get frustrated voters,

00:28:20.970 --> 00:28:22.923
you're not gonna get into this crazy,

00:28:23.990 --> 00:28:24.823
very American,

00:28:24.823 --> 00:28:26.330
all-or-nothing mentality.

00:28:26.330 --> 00:28:29.600
Either we throw the
entire US military at it,

00:28:29.600 --> 00:28:31.963
or we do nothing at all, right?

00:28:33.070 --> 00:28:35.100
This doesn't work domestically.

00:28:35.100 --> 00:28:37.590
If you were working with a,

00:28:37.590 --> 00:28:38.910
I don't know,

00:28:38.910 --> 00:28:42.410
a community in the United
States with high unemployment,

00:28:42.410 --> 00:28:44.723
let's say a coal community
in East Kentucky.

00:28:45.720 --> 00:28:47.770
You wouldn't think that
it makes sense to say,

00:28:47.770 --> 00:28:49.629
we're gonna go and we're
gonna to fix everything

00:28:49.629 --> 00:28:51.570
and we're going to do
it by dumping, you know,

00:28:51.570 --> 00:28:53.290
a hundred billion dollars on you.

00:28:53.290 --> 00:28:56.570
And then if it doesn't work
out in 10 years, we're leaving.

00:28:56.570 --> 00:28:57.700
We've got an exit strategy,

00:28:57.700 --> 00:28:58.920
and then we'll blame you.

00:28:58.920 --> 00:29:00.060
We'll say you're all cowardly

00:29:00.060 --> 00:29:02.313
and incompetent and corrupt and we're off.

00:29:03.390 --> 00:29:07.960
Of course, partnering is a
very long, patient process.

00:29:07.960 --> 00:29:10.060
And parts of the thing we did

00:29:10.060 --> 00:29:12.380
is the Afghans became frustrated

00:29:12.380 --> 00:29:16.430
because we pretended to
do things we couldn't do.

00:29:16.430 --> 00:29:18.326
Instead of being honest from the beginning

00:29:18.326 --> 00:29:20.270
that it was going to be messy,

00:29:20.270 --> 00:29:23.067
that it was going to be very
long-term and getting out.

00:29:23.067 --> 00:29:24.840
And I think Orzala was wonderful on this.

00:29:24.840 --> 00:29:27.990
The ridiculous, you know,

00:29:27.990 --> 00:29:30.740
hero to villain thing that
we did with the presidents,

00:29:30.740 --> 00:29:33.550
with President Karzai
and Present Ghani, right?

00:29:33.550 --> 00:29:34.660
Actually in many ways,

00:29:34.660 --> 00:29:36.300
compared to presidents

00:29:36.300 --> 00:29:37.710
and much the rest of developing world,

00:29:37.710 --> 00:29:39.400
these were pretty good guys.

00:29:39.400 --> 00:29:42.150
The problem was that we would
convince ourself one moment

00:29:42.150 --> 00:29:44.110
that they were geniuses and saints,

00:29:44.110 --> 00:29:45.313
and then the next moment we all said

00:29:45.313 --> 00:29:47.693
that they were corrupt and incompetent.

00:29:48.860 --> 00:29:51.060
It's a crazy way of
thinking about anything.

00:29:52.220 --> 00:29:54.637
- Really powerful lessons
being shared here.

00:29:54.637 --> 00:29:56.830
We have some questions coming in,

00:29:56.830 --> 00:29:58.130
and many of these questions

00:29:58.130 --> 00:29:59.920
are geared toward Afghanistan's future.

00:29:59.920 --> 00:30:02.470
So I wanna make sure we
move in that direction.

00:30:02.470 --> 00:30:03.560
But first, you know,

00:30:03.560 --> 00:30:06.231
some of this has been brought up,

00:30:06.231 --> 00:30:10.900
a crisis in terms of
food security, hunger,

00:30:10.900 --> 00:30:13.220
but as we all know,

00:30:13.220 --> 00:30:15.090
three months now into Taliban rule,

00:30:15.090 --> 00:30:17.570
the country is really facing
a humanitarian crisis,

00:30:17.570 --> 00:30:21.398
from its economy to
issues related to hunger.

00:30:21.398 --> 00:30:23.488
I just wanna hear from each of you,

00:30:23.488 --> 00:30:25.430
sort of what are your top concerns

00:30:25.430 --> 00:30:27.190
when it comes to the
state of the country now,

00:30:27.190 --> 00:30:28.500
and that will help us transition

00:30:28.500 --> 00:30:31.291
into the role the international
development community

00:30:31.291 --> 00:30:32.880
can and should play,

00:30:32.880 --> 00:30:35.390
and perhaps what they should stop doing.

00:30:35.390 --> 00:30:37.470
So, as was mentioned earlier,

00:30:37.470 --> 00:30:38.870
before the Taliban takeover,

00:30:38.870 --> 00:30:42.100
the country received an
enormous amount of foreign aid,

00:30:42.100 --> 00:30:43.700
or as Orzala mentioned earlier,

00:30:43.700 --> 00:30:46.980
this problem of donor dependency.

00:30:46.980 --> 00:30:48.000
So perhaps that's something

00:30:48.000 --> 00:30:50.010
we can bring into the
conversation as well.

00:30:50.010 --> 00:30:52.380
Like how did the country end up here?

00:30:52.380 --> 00:30:55.600
And it's not just the
very messy withdrawal

00:30:55.600 --> 00:30:56.550
that is part of it,

00:30:56.550 --> 00:30:58.210
but there are other
dynamics at play as well.

00:30:58.210 --> 00:31:00.260
So what are your top concerns

00:31:00.260 --> 00:31:03.090
in terms of the crisis
the country's facing now?

00:31:03.090 --> 00:31:03.923
And then we'll get into

00:31:03.923 --> 00:31:05.790
what the international
development community can do.

00:31:05.790 --> 00:31:07.290
Maybe we'll start with Khalid?

00:31:08.720 --> 00:31:10.460
- Yeah, absolutely.

00:31:10.460 --> 00:31:12.511
So just to put it in context,

00:31:12.511 --> 00:31:14.753
Afghanistan received 40% of,

00:31:15.917 --> 00:31:20.740
compared to 40% of the GDP in aid.

00:31:20.740 --> 00:31:25.740
50% of the government's
budget was financed by donors.

00:31:25.780 --> 00:31:26.690
And that meant, you know,

00:31:26.690 --> 00:31:31.520
in a budget where 75% of the
budget goes to operating,

00:31:31.520 --> 00:31:35.530
you know, salaries of police
and teachers and civil service,

00:31:35.530 --> 00:31:37.660
you know, half of it was by by donors

00:31:37.660 --> 00:31:41.830
and everything, the same amount
was of budget, $8 billion.

00:31:41.830 --> 00:31:44.890
Suddenly, it's like a
tap turned off overnight.

00:31:44.890 --> 00:31:49.590
It's going to create a massive
problem, and it already has.

00:31:49.590 --> 00:31:50.460
And on top of it,

00:31:50.460 --> 00:31:54.260
you have a banking sector that
you rely on more and more,

00:31:54.260 --> 00:31:56.960
not for lending normal banking

00:31:56.960 --> 00:31:59.650
because we had issues in that area,

00:31:59.650 --> 00:32:01.260
but for transfers.

00:32:01.260 --> 00:32:03.620
So the meager savings that people had,

00:32:03.620 --> 00:32:06.720
the equivalent to a few hundred dollars

00:32:06.720 --> 00:32:11.720
were stuck in banks that they
could not access for weeks.

00:32:12.230 --> 00:32:13.950
And it created a massive problem,

00:32:13.950 --> 00:32:16.466
massive problem in urban centers,

00:32:16.466 --> 00:32:21.010
which was already seeing
higher levels of poverty levels

00:32:21.010 --> 00:32:25.460
than rural sectors,
because they were dependent

00:32:25.460 --> 00:32:27.680
more on the government than others.

00:32:27.680 --> 00:32:30.180
So my biggest concern is the famine.

00:32:30.180 --> 00:32:33.110
In June and July, I was
still at the government.

00:32:33.110 --> 00:32:36.711
We launched a humanitarian appeal,

00:32:36.711 --> 00:32:38.760
a joint one with the UN,

00:32:38.760 --> 00:32:42.001
because we were facing
problems with drought,

00:32:42.001 --> 00:32:45.270
problems with displacement
and problems with COVID.

00:32:45.270 --> 00:32:48.134
The third wave of COVID was very harsh

00:32:48.134 --> 00:32:53.132
and it had meant that
12 to 18 million people,

00:32:53.132 --> 00:32:55.790
half of the population then,

00:32:55.790 --> 00:32:57.040
were at the risk of it.

00:32:57.040 --> 00:32:59.664
And now you add on top of it

00:32:59.664 --> 00:33:02.820
the sudden reduction or stoppage of aid,

00:33:02.820 --> 00:33:04.760
that's a huge catastrophe.

00:33:04.760 --> 00:33:06.218
My biggest concern is

00:33:06.218 --> 00:33:08.930
that I don't see, unfortunately,

00:33:08.930 --> 00:33:12.293
the urgency from
international organizations.

00:33:12.293 --> 00:33:15.070
Everybody's looking to the US treasury

00:33:15.070 --> 00:33:18.510
and the US treasury does
not either care or answer

00:33:18.510 --> 00:33:19.760
or they're afraid of Biden

00:33:19.760 --> 00:33:23.060
because he does not want
to hear Afghanistan's name,

00:33:23.060 --> 00:33:24.890
to put it very bluntly.

00:33:24.890 --> 00:33:27.610
And other international organizations,

00:33:27.610 --> 00:33:29.275
because they have powerful governors

00:33:29.275 --> 00:33:32.510
of the US government
sitting on their boards,

00:33:32.510 --> 00:33:34.350
they're all paralyzed, you know.

00:33:34.350 --> 00:33:37.740
The World Bank sits on the
biggest trust fund in the world

00:33:37.740 --> 00:33:38.573
for Afghanistan.

00:33:38.573 --> 00:33:40.110
Afghanistan reconstruction trust fund,

00:33:40.110 --> 00:33:43.320
you have to just convert
the ordinary construction

00:33:43.320 --> 00:33:44.672
into relief and you know,

00:33:44.672 --> 00:33:46.710
reprogram some of them,

00:33:46.710 --> 00:33:48.490
but it's not happening.

00:33:48.490 --> 00:33:49.970
My concern is,

00:33:49.970 --> 00:33:53.560
taking time on this when we
are approaching the winter

00:33:53.560 --> 00:33:58.440
is going to cause massive human suffering,

00:33:58.440 --> 00:34:00.436
maybe something that we haven't seen,

00:34:00.436 --> 00:34:02.613
at least not this century,

00:34:03.769 --> 00:34:05.520
maybe in the last 40, 50 years.

00:34:05.520 --> 00:34:08.420
35 million people,

00:34:08.420 --> 00:34:11.410
where UN is projecting 97% poverty

00:34:11.410 --> 00:34:13.060
by the end of this year,

00:34:13.060 --> 00:34:14.900
that's unheard of anywhere.

00:34:14.900 --> 00:34:17.500
And you know, as somebody
who's worked in this field,

00:34:17.500 --> 00:34:20.600
I can tell you that these
are not exaggerated numbers.

00:34:20.600 --> 00:34:24.190
97% could be, you know,

00:34:24.190 --> 00:34:25.023
the base case.

00:34:25.023 --> 00:34:27.890
It could get worse than this.

00:34:27.890 --> 00:34:31.840
I think humanitarian
aid needs to move fast.

00:34:31.840 --> 00:34:34.710
I think the international community,

00:34:34.710 --> 00:34:37.020
international donors,

00:34:37.020 --> 00:34:40.380
the UN and the IFIs who've
been engaged in Afghanistan

00:34:40.380 --> 00:34:41.570
and who've partnered

00:34:41.570 --> 00:34:46.570
have to find a way around
the US treasury sanctions,

00:34:49.240 --> 00:34:52.070
because those are not going
to be lifted any time soon.

00:34:52.070 --> 00:34:55.100
If you are a realist
you have to think that.

00:34:55.100 --> 00:34:56.320
But there are other ways,

00:34:56.320 --> 00:34:58.480
mechanisms to swapping

00:34:58.480 --> 00:35:02.110
and mechanisms through physical transfer

00:35:02.110 --> 00:35:03.300
of cash into the country

00:35:03.300 --> 00:35:06.600
to make sure that these programs go ahead.

00:35:06.600 --> 00:35:07.770
And talking of the gains,

00:35:07.770 --> 00:35:11.640
some of these achievements
that were made in Afghanistan

00:35:11.640 --> 00:35:16.310
were done in collaboration
with international agencies,

00:35:16.310 --> 00:35:17.950
but also NGOs.

00:35:17.950 --> 00:35:19.784
The basic package of health services

00:35:19.784 --> 00:35:22.030
was all delivered to NGOs.

00:35:22.030 --> 00:35:24.540
The community development councils

00:35:24.540 --> 00:35:27.790
by the sub-national levels.

00:35:27.790 --> 00:35:28.720
You know,

00:35:28.720 --> 00:35:32.818
you don't need to go to
a Taliban administration,

00:35:32.818 --> 00:35:35.060
a UN agency, for example,

00:35:35.060 --> 00:35:39.260
WHO could take over the
coordination and invoicing

00:35:39.260 --> 00:35:42.470
and messaging oversight mechanisms

00:35:42.470 --> 00:35:44.290
that was done by ministry
of public health,

00:35:44.290 --> 00:35:46.410
and you could still still do this.

00:35:46.410 --> 00:35:49.500
So my concern is humanitarian plus.

00:35:49.500 --> 00:35:50.910
By humanitarian plus,

00:35:50.910 --> 00:35:53.530
I mean basic services
of health and education

00:35:53.530 --> 00:35:55.540
and community development must flow.

00:35:55.540 --> 00:36:00.540
Otherwise, we will have
suffering at the large scale.

00:36:00.770 --> 00:36:03.040
I think just my last point,

00:36:03.040 --> 00:36:07.700
something that Rory alluded
to in his opening points,

00:36:07.700 --> 00:36:09.707
you also have to understand Taliban.

00:36:09.707 --> 00:36:13.270
Leverages like this do not work on them.

00:36:13.270 --> 00:36:16.660
You are creating suffering
for a lot of people.

00:36:16.660 --> 00:36:18.050
They don't care about it, you know.

00:36:18.050 --> 00:36:20.450
They could still rule with an iron fist

00:36:20.450 --> 00:36:21.830
for the next 20 years.

00:36:21.830 --> 00:36:26.563
And there is a limit to how
much aid could have overall.

00:36:27.610 --> 00:36:28.960
So please have,

00:36:28.960 --> 00:36:32.150
look at my appeal to
international community as,

00:36:32.150 --> 00:36:33.430
look at the bigger picture,

00:36:33.430 --> 00:36:36.010
look at the suffering of a lot of people.

00:36:36.010 --> 00:36:37.960
And it's not just Afghanistan,

00:36:37.960 --> 00:36:40.417
it's going to haunt the whole world.

00:36:40.417 --> 00:36:41.250
You know Afghanistan,

00:36:41.250 --> 00:36:44.740
fortunately or unfortunately
will always be in headlines.

00:36:44.740 --> 00:36:48.200
And if you leave it like
they have right now,

00:36:48.200 --> 00:36:49.780
they think it's the end of it, it's not,

00:36:49.780 --> 00:36:53.780
it's going to come back and bite everyone.

00:36:53.780 --> 00:36:54.700
- So many great points

00:36:54.700 --> 00:36:56.790
and a couple of things I want
to return to in a moment,

00:36:56.790 --> 00:36:59.110
but I wanna bring Dr. Orzala in here.

00:36:59.110 --> 00:37:03.000
But you mentioned it was like
turning the tap off overnight.

00:37:03.000 --> 00:37:05.790
And as you said, it's not
just the foreign aid dollars,

00:37:05.790 --> 00:37:07.760
but the NGOs working on the ground,

00:37:07.760 --> 00:37:10.070
many of whom have pulled out their staff,

00:37:10.070 --> 00:37:12.480
or are still trying to
figure out how to engage.

00:37:12.480 --> 00:37:14.947
One of the things I
also appreciate Khalid,

00:37:14.947 --> 00:37:18.767
in addition to just outlining
the crisis and the urgency,

00:37:18.767 --> 00:37:21.790
you mentioned that there
are practical challenges,

00:37:21.790 --> 00:37:25.880
but also ways to find
a way around sanctions.

00:37:25.880 --> 00:37:27.880
And I do hope we can kind of
get into quite practically,

00:37:27.880 --> 00:37:30.220
how does international
development community engage.

00:37:30.220 --> 00:37:31.780
But first, Dr. Orzala,

00:37:31.780 --> 00:37:32.820
I know you wanted to jump in,

00:37:32.820 --> 00:37:34.990
just in terms of outlining
where the country is now.

00:37:34.990 --> 00:37:37.840
And then we'll kind of move
into where things are headed.

00:37:39.340 --> 00:37:41.930
- Just to add in terms of, you know,

00:37:41.930 --> 00:37:43.773
humanitarian crisis.

00:37:44.617 --> 00:37:47.210
The urgency is critical,

00:37:47.210 --> 00:37:50.560
as we mentioned throughout
the conversation here.

00:37:50.560 --> 00:37:55.020
But I liked the point Khalid
made regarding, you know,

00:37:55.020 --> 00:37:56.320
humanitarian plus

00:37:56.320 --> 00:38:00.975
because the goal here should
not be to keep Afghanistan.

00:38:00.975 --> 00:38:03.990
We have a saying in Afghanistan,

00:38:03.990 --> 00:38:05.440
that serum, you know,

00:38:05.440 --> 00:38:07.770
you go to the doctor and immediately

00:38:07.770 --> 00:38:10.340
he puts an injection and a serum into you.

00:38:10.340 --> 00:38:12.170
We don't want our country,

00:38:12.170 --> 00:38:15.929
38 million of people to become dependent

00:38:15.929 --> 00:38:18.780
on the aid and food.

00:38:18.780 --> 00:38:21.730
So yes, feed people now

00:38:21.730 --> 00:38:24.237
because a terrible winter is on its way,

00:38:24.237 --> 00:38:26.740
and people don't have enough
to keep themselves warm,

00:38:26.740 --> 00:38:29.840
to keep their children and themselves fed.

00:38:29.840 --> 00:38:32.420
So this is immediate emergency.

00:38:32.420 --> 00:38:35.360
And I call not only on donors,

00:38:35.360 --> 00:38:37.300
and organizations and government,

00:38:37.300 --> 00:38:38.750
but also on individuals,

00:38:38.750 --> 00:38:41.640
anyone who knows someone
in Afghanistan just should,

00:38:41.640 --> 00:38:44.750
all you need is a bank account
and an ID from someone.

00:38:44.750 --> 00:38:46.090
Transfer some dollars,

00:38:46.090 --> 00:38:47.363
transfer your lunch money,

00:38:47.363 --> 00:38:50.300
just be on a diet for one
day and get something,

00:38:50.300 --> 00:38:51.190
send something there,

00:38:51.190 --> 00:38:52.980
because really everyone needs it.

00:38:52.980 --> 00:38:53.910
Even people,

00:38:53.910 --> 00:38:56.295
I'm telling this to the
middle class type of people,

00:38:56.295 --> 00:38:58.630
even people who had savings,

00:38:58.630 --> 00:39:01.440
they saved a lot of money for
the future of their children,

00:39:01.440 --> 00:39:02.610
like you all will do,

00:39:02.610 --> 00:39:04.480
the audience of this panel.

00:39:04.480 --> 00:39:06.430
It's all frozen in their accounts,

00:39:06.430 --> 00:39:07.450
they cannot take it out.

00:39:07.450 --> 00:39:12.199
So this is a kind of a human
responsibility to all of us.

00:39:12.199 --> 00:39:14.723
The issue is that this should not be

00:39:14.723 --> 00:39:16.073
a long-term solution.

00:39:16.073 --> 00:39:17.844
The development assistance,

00:39:17.844 --> 00:39:21.170
the sustainability of getting people,

00:39:21.170 --> 00:39:23.800
Afghan people are entrepreneurs by nature.

00:39:23.800 --> 00:39:25.730
All they need is some means.

00:39:25.730 --> 00:39:28.360
So that should be really also critical.

00:39:28.360 --> 00:39:30.905
To keep the development assistance,

00:39:30.905 --> 00:39:31.814
the development aid,

00:39:31.814 --> 00:39:33.387
the development of,

00:39:33.387 --> 00:39:35.350
the economic development specifically

00:39:35.350 --> 00:39:38.500
is something that we have
to think about longer-term.

00:39:38.500 --> 00:39:41.430
I'll later probably come in
a moment on the sanctions,

00:39:41.430 --> 00:39:42.850
but stop here, thanks.

00:39:42.850 --> 00:39:43.690
- Yeah, we'll return to that.

00:39:43.690 --> 00:39:44.936
Absolutely.

00:39:44.936 --> 00:39:46.300
And the point you just made

00:39:46.300 --> 00:39:47.800
kind of gets back to what Rory was saying

00:39:47.800 --> 00:39:50.110
in terms of small and
medium enterprises, SMEs.

00:39:50.110 --> 00:39:51.810
But Rory, did you want to jump in?

00:39:54.320 --> 00:39:55.790
- I think the first thing is that I think

00:39:55.790 --> 00:39:59.230
all of us agree that the
international community

00:39:59.230 --> 00:40:00.960
should be generous towards Afghanistan.

00:40:00.960 --> 00:40:01.870
And now,

00:40:01.870 --> 00:40:03.870
that means getting humanitarian
assistance sent out,

00:40:03.870 --> 00:40:06.763
but it also means continuing
development assistance.

00:40:07.880 --> 00:40:09.680
Not just emergency food aid,

00:40:09.680 --> 00:40:11.980
but the things that help
the Afghan economy grow

00:40:11.980 --> 00:40:14.178
and help the Afghan
community support itself.

00:40:14.178 --> 00:40:16.310
Why is it not happening?

00:40:16.310 --> 00:40:18.090
It's not happening for
a series of reasons.

00:40:18.090 --> 00:40:19.970
One of them, unfortunately

00:40:19.970 --> 00:40:22.410
is there is a sense of emotional anger

00:40:22.410 --> 00:40:25.460
and humiliation from the
international community.

00:40:25.460 --> 00:40:27.420
So when I say on Twitter, for example,

00:40:27.420 --> 00:40:28.810
please support Afghanistan

00:40:28.810 --> 00:40:32.120
I get people tweeting
back saying nonsense stuff

00:40:32.120 --> 00:40:34.500
like, "They chose the
Taliban, it's their fault.

00:40:34.500 --> 00:40:36.114
Why should we help them now?"

00:40:36.114 --> 00:40:38.940
So we have to be honest with people

00:40:38.940 --> 00:40:40.840
about the fact that they're wrong

00:40:40.840 --> 00:40:43.593
in saying that Afghans
chose the Taliban, right?

00:40:44.840 --> 00:40:47.220
The other thing is that people believe

00:40:47.220 --> 00:40:48.560
or are trying to convince themselves,

00:40:48.560 --> 00:40:49.720
particularly politicians,

00:40:49.720 --> 00:40:51.330
that somehow this is gonna give people

00:40:51.330 --> 00:40:53.210
leverage over the Taliban.

00:40:53.210 --> 00:40:56.560
Well, this is again wrong.

00:40:56.560 --> 00:40:58.330
It's wrong in many ways.

00:40:58.330 --> 00:41:00.630
As Khalid pointed out,
it just doesn't work.

00:41:00.630 --> 00:41:02.200
Many of these Taliban fighters

00:41:02.200 --> 00:41:03.770
have been living in very tough conditions,

00:41:03.770 --> 00:41:06.900
in refugee camps or almost
in caves in the hills

00:41:06.900 --> 00:41:07.740
in some situations.

00:41:07.740 --> 00:41:09.480
They're not like Russian oligarchs.

00:41:09.480 --> 00:41:10.620
They don't want to go shopping

00:41:10.620 --> 00:41:13.360
in fifth avenue in Manhattan, right?

00:41:13.360 --> 00:41:15.760
So they're not primarily motivated

00:41:15.760 --> 00:41:17.550
by these questions around sanctions.

00:41:17.550 --> 00:41:18.902
But there's a bigger point,

00:41:18.902 --> 00:41:22.990
which is that actually it's morally wrong

00:41:22.990 --> 00:41:24.960
to starve the Afghan people,

00:41:24.960 --> 00:41:27.520
believing that by threatening
to starve the Afghan people

00:41:27.520 --> 00:41:29.410
you're gonna somehow get
political concessions

00:41:29.410 --> 00:41:30.790
out of the Taliban

00:41:30.790 --> 00:41:32.713
is totally immoral.

00:41:33.704 --> 00:41:36.400
Now, what does this mean practically?

00:41:36.400 --> 00:41:38.260
Practically, a lot of
this is bureaucratic.

00:41:38.260 --> 00:41:41.130
This is, why is the money stopping?

00:41:41.130 --> 00:41:43.250
It's stopping often for
small bureaucratic reasons.

00:41:43.250 --> 00:41:47.480
State department has suddenly
canceled a lot of its projects

00:41:47.480 --> 00:41:49.440
because they said they
didn't have enough staff

00:41:49.440 --> 00:41:51.220
on the ground to administer them.

00:41:51.220 --> 00:41:53.470
European Union has
canceled a lot of projects

00:41:53.470 --> 00:41:54.830
saying we don't do development anymore,

00:41:54.830 --> 00:41:56.680
we're now just gonna do humanitarian.

00:41:58.080 --> 00:42:01.790
USAID is trying to re-profile, right?

00:42:01.790 --> 00:42:03.790
Which is a very complicated,
bureaucratic process

00:42:03.790 --> 00:42:04.623
where they're saying

00:42:04.623 --> 00:42:06.230
we don't want to support export industry.

00:42:06.230 --> 00:42:07.300
It's like the carpet industry,

00:42:07.300 --> 00:42:09.320
but we're going to go into livelihoods.

00:42:09.320 --> 00:42:10.657
And then other people are saying,

00:42:10.657 --> 00:42:13.587
"No, there are terrorists who
are in the Afghan government."

00:42:13.587 --> 00:42:16.070
And that's the most dangerous thing of all

00:42:16.070 --> 00:42:17.904
because people are
beginning to say, "No, but",

00:42:17.904 --> 00:42:19.953
this is the sanctions people.

00:42:20.980 --> 00:42:22.470
Let's say you export a carpet.

00:42:22.470 --> 00:42:24.630
Let's say you pay 2% export tax

00:42:24.630 --> 00:42:26.387
and that export tax goes
to the Afghan government,

00:42:26.387 --> 00:42:28.027
and the Afghan government
is a Taliban government

00:42:28.027 --> 00:42:30.200
and one of the ministers is a terrorist,

00:42:30.200 --> 00:42:32.360
are you "funding" terrorists?

00:42:32.360 --> 00:42:35.895
If you buy petrol in the gas station

00:42:35.895 --> 00:42:37.710
and you pay sales tax

00:42:37.710 --> 00:42:39.330
and that money goes to
the Afghan government

00:42:39.330 --> 00:42:40.537
and the Afghan governments
is the Taliban government,

00:42:40.537 --> 00:42:41.850
and a member of the cabinet's a terrorist,

00:42:41.850 --> 00:42:43.363
are you "funding" terrorim?

00:42:45.253 --> 00:42:46.120
So the international community

00:42:46.120 --> 00:42:49.210
needs to be clear and practical

00:42:49.210 --> 00:42:50.580
and take a little bit of risk

00:42:50.580 --> 00:42:51.760
and make some decisions.

00:42:51.760 --> 00:42:52.982
And the decision has to be,

00:42:52.982 --> 00:42:55.120
be quick, be generous,

00:42:55.120 --> 00:42:59.143
and don't get caught up in
these problems around sanctions.

00:43:00.700 --> 00:43:01.533
- Thank you, Rory.

00:43:01.533 --> 00:43:03.270
We have a lot of questions
coming in from the audience,

00:43:03.270 --> 00:43:05.740
but first I wanted to
see if Orzala or Khalid

00:43:05.740 --> 00:43:07.460
wanted to respond to that at all?

00:43:07.460 --> 00:43:10.210
Otherwise, I definitely have
some questions to turn to.

00:43:12.290 --> 00:43:14.120
Go ahead, Orzala.

00:43:14.120 --> 00:43:17.312
- I just wanted to very briefly reiterate

00:43:17.312 --> 00:43:19.910
on the sanction point that Rory has made,

00:43:19.910 --> 00:43:21.480
and then we can go to questions.

00:43:21.480 --> 00:43:24.980
My personal view on the sanction situation

00:43:24.980 --> 00:43:28.010
is that I would like
to know about a country

00:43:28.010 --> 00:43:29.880
where sanction have achieved the goal

00:43:29.880 --> 00:43:32.210
that the country is trying
to punish economically,

00:43:32.210 --> 00:43:33.760
those countries have achieved.

00:43:33.760 --> 00:43:34.593
And then I would say,

00:43:34.593 --> 00:43:35.760
yes this is the best solution.

00:43:35.760 --> 00:43:36.920
I mean, emotions aside,

00:43:36.920 --> 00:43:39.661
everyone is angry and upset
in one way or another,

00:43:39.661 --> 00:43:43.360
but here it's not about
me and what I lost.

00:43:43.360 --> 00:43:46.300
It's about 30 million people,

00:43:46.300 --> 00:43:48.270
6 million of whom are living in Kabul,

00:43:48.270 --> 00:43:52.710
one of the most highly
population-concentrated cities

00:43:52.710 --> 00:43:54.100
in the country,

00:43:54.100 --> 00:43:55.930
and they just don't know what to do next.

00:43:55.930 --> 00:43:58.090
The urban poverty is as much deep

00:43:58.090 --> 00:44:01.030
and as much dangerous
as the rural poverty.

00:44:01.030 --> 00:44:03.730
The rural is a little bit
less at risk at this time

00:44:03.730 --> 00:44:06.350
because they have been like
going through this poverty

00:44:06.350 --> 00:44:07.370
for a long, long time.

00:44:07.370 --> 00:44:09.420
But even people with, as I said,

00:44:09.420 --> 00:44:11.990
small and medium sort of
activities and enterprises

00:44:11.990 --> 00:44:13.450
are going into crisis.

00:44:13.450 --> 00:44:15.023
So, the question,

00:44:15.023 --> 00:44:16.930
there has to be a distinction

00:44:16.930 --> 00:44:20.040
between giving legitimacy to some actions

00:44:20.040 --> 00:44:22.755
that the Taliban government are taking,

00:44:22.755 --> 00:44:25.900
particularly their gender
apartheid at the moment,

00:44:25.900 --> 00:44:26.733
in terms of, you know,

00:44:26.733 --> 00:44:29.070
complete discrimination against women.

00:44:29.070 --> 00:44:30.120
They have to really,

00:44:30.120 --> 00:44:32.070
and just share with you,

00:44:32.070 --> 00:44:34.620
I mean, some sort of
reflections from the ground

00:44:35.577 --> 00:44:36.650
within the Taliban,

00:44:36.650 --> 00:44:40.420
within the Taliban
younger generation forces,

00:44:40.420 --> 00:44:42.930
they themselves are not convinced.

00:44:42.930 --> 00:44:44.300
And I'm sure they're listening to me

00:44:44.300 --> 00:44:45.690
saying that they are not convinced

00:44:45.690 --> 00:44:48.360
with these actions of
closing girls' education.

00:44:48.360 --> 00:44:50.370
because I'm sure their own sisters at home

00:44:50.370 --> 00:44:52.800
are not happy with this decision.

00:44:52.800 --> 00:44:55.600
If their sisters are living
in Afghanistan at the moment,

00:44:55.600 --> 00:44:58.240
which I'm sure for some
of them it's the case.

00:44:58.240 --> 00:45:00.650
If they are in the middle
of their universities,

00:45:00.650 --> 00:45:02.250
they cannot attend the universities,

00:45:02.250 --> 00:45:04.310
they are also not confident.

00:45:04.310 --> 00:45:05.143
And they're already,

00:45:05.143 --> 00:45:07.830
like somebody was sharing on social media,

00:45:07.830 --> 00:45:09.300
a picture of a drive-through

00:45:09.300 --> 00:45:11.360
which shows girls going to school

00:45:11.360 --> 00:45:13.590
and immediately one of the young Taliban

00:45:13.590 --> 00:45:14.840
tweeted in Arabic and says,

00:45:14.840 --> 00:45:16.880
this is rumors against the Taliban

00:45:16.880 --> 00:45:19.530
that girls are not
letting to go to school,

00:45:19.530 --> 00:45:21.910
here, look, they are going to school.

00:45:21.910 --> 00:45:24.290
And that guy again, corrected himself,

00:45:24.290 --> 00:45:26.130
says no, this is primary school,

00:45:26.130 --> 00:45:27.279
these are not children.

00:45:27.279 --> 00:45:29.420
These are not secondary and high school

00:45:29.420 --> 00:45:30.880
and university students,

00:45:30.880 --> 00:45:32.500
girls are still not going.

00:45:32.500 --> 00:45:33.940
So scholars,

00:45:33.940 --> 00:45:35.890
Islamic scholars from around the world

00:45:35.890 --> 00:45:37.450
called on Taliban and says,

00:45:37.450 --> 00:45:39.360
can you come into a conversation with us

00:45:39.360 --> 00:45:41.270
so that we can resolve this?

00:45:41.270 --> 00:45:45.300
My view is that the
issue of girls education

00:45:45.300 --> 00:45:48.560
is not related to international
lobbying and advocacy.

00:45:48.560 --> 00:45:51.440
I think the people of
Afghanistan themselves

00:45:51.440 --> 00:45:54.050
will manage their way to convince Taliban

00:45:54.050 --> 00:45:59.050
to move on from this very
illogical sort of step

00:45:59.740 --> 00:46:02.430
to keep girls away from education

00:46:02.430 --> 00:46:04.580
and to keep women away from work.

00:46:04.580 --> 00:46:06.545
Practically sector by sector,

00:46:06.545 --> 00:46:10.370
we observe some flexibility.

00:46:10.370 --> 00:46:12.180
Health sector was first.

00:46:12.180 --> 00:46:14.950
They never had restrictions on
doctors attending the clinics

00:46:14.950 --> 00:46:16.010
or nurses,

00:46:16.010 --> 00:46:18.190
but slowly vaccination, slowly others.

00:46:18.190 --> 00:46:21.170
So there has to be ongoing engagement.

00:46:21.170 --> 00:46:24.960
Dehumanization has not
worked, will not work.

00:46:24.960 --> 00:46:27.006
So there has to be ongoing engagement.

00:46:27.006 --> 00:46:29.120
Sanctions from the global,

00:46:29.120 --> 00:46:30.510
from international point of view

00:46:30.510 --> 00:46:33.271
is completely problematic as an Afghan.

00:46:33.271 --> 00:46:35.560
I think it's extremely problematic

00:46:35.560 --> 00:46:38.950
in that the harm of it will
go to the average Afghan

00:46:38.950 --> 00:46:41.590
living in Zabul, Oruzgan, you know,

00:46:41.590 --> 00:46:42.640
Nuristan, Kunar,

00:46:42.640 --> 00:46:44.570
everywhere in the country,

00:46:44.570 --> 00:46:47.290
or Hazarajat, everywhere basically.

00:46:47.290 --> 00:46:48.810
It will not affect the Taliban,

00:46:48.810 --> 00:46:49.643
It will not,

00:46:49.643 --> 00:46:52.280
because let's not forget the role

00:46:52.280 --> 00:46:54.590
of illicit economy in the country.

00:46:54.590 --> 00:46:57.190
Other secondary sources
that are not on the record,

00:46:57.190 --> 00:47:00.360
but still feed very well this
kind of government and systems

00:47:00.360 --> 00:47:01.993
and those in power.

00:47:02.936 --> 00:47:04.440
- Orzala, I wanna bring in one question

00:47:04.440 --> 00:47:06.053
from the audience for you.

00:47:06.053 --> 00:47:06.930
And you may have just touched on this,

00:47:06.930 --> 00:47:08.910
but in case there's anything to add,

00:47:08.910 --> 00:47:09.743
the question is,

00:47:09.743 --> 00:47:10.576
what is your outlook

00:47:10.576 --> 00:47:12.870
regarding women and
girls under the Taliban?

00:47:12.870 --> 00:47:16.443
What is your biggest concern
and any ideas for solutions?

00:47:18.220 --> 00:47:19.207
- My outlook,

00:47:19.207 --> 00:47:23.880
despite going through a
very, very harsh situation

00:47:23.880 --> 00:47:25.340
at the moment in terms of, you know,

00:47:25.340 --> 00:47:26.520
women and girls' education.

00:47:26.520 --> 00:47:27.560
And I have like hundreds

00:47:27.560 --> 00:47:31.240
of women and girls
basically contacting me,

00:47:31.240 --> 00:47:32.940
and I'm sure many other women leaders

00:47:32.940 --> 00:47:34.650
at what will be the next.

00:47:34.650 --> 00:47:35.911
I am personally optimistic

00:47:35.911 --> 00:47:37.900
that this will be very temporary

00:47:37.900 --> 00:47:41.738
and there will be changes
and pressure on Taliban.

00:47:41.738 --> 00:47:44.850
I'm not necessarily talking
only about international,

00:47:44.850 --> 00:47:47.490
international pressure
should also be consistent.

00:47:47.490 --> 00:47:51.017
But national pressure is
consistent on Taliban,

00:47:51.017 --> 00:47:54.820
to come into terms of allowing education,

00:47:54.820 --> 00:47:58.020
allowing women to work
in different sectors,

00:47:58.020 --> 00:48:00.550
and even allowing women
to work also in politics.

00:48:00.550 --> 00:48:02.450
So the concern,

00:48:02.450 --> 00:48:06.570
the biggest concern is to
just see some countries,

00:48:06.570 --> 00:48:10.280
major countries with a lot
of leverage and influences,

00:48:10.280 --> 00:48:12.470
try to just keep a close eye

00:48:12.470 --> 00:48:15.640
on all the actions of the
Taliban at national level

00:48:15.640 --> 00:48:17.750
and just move on by legitimizing them.

00:48:17.750 --> 00:48:21.309
So there is a very
narrow sort of line here,

00:48:21.309 --> 00:48:23.163
not to be crossed.

00:48:24.000 --> 00:48:27.130
Engage yes, but not
legitimize their actions

00:48:27.130 --> 00:48:28.870
so that they don't get overconfident

00:48:28.870 --> 00:48:31.060
by some of the wrongdoings that they do

00:48:31.060 --> 00:48:33.340
in terms of, you know, moving forward.

00:48:33.340 --> 00:48:38.040
So continuous engagement
is key to move forward.

00:48:38.040 --> 00:48:40.230
Concern is to see this falling down.

00:48:40.230 --> 00:48:43.200
Afghanistan should
remain in conversations,

00:48:43.200 --> 00:48:46.070
but in constructive conversations,

00:48:46.070 --> 00:48:47.110
not really something

00:48:47.110 --> 00:48:49.730
that will continue to
divide us and rule us.

00:48:49.730 --> 00:48:51.060
Thank you.

00:48:51.060 --> 00:48:51.893
- Thank you.

00:48:51.893 --> 00:48:54.230
So speaking of this
concern of legitimizing,

00:48:54.230 --> 00:48:56.130
there's a question here

00:48:56.130 --> 00:48:57.490
kind of broadening the lens

00:48:57.490 --> 00:48:59.170
in terms of the international community's

00:48:59.170 --> 00:49:00.460
future engagement with Afghanistan.

00:49:00.460 --> 00:49:01.730
Here's the question from our audience,

00:49:01.730 --> 00:49:03.160
building on some of what's been discussed,

00:49:03.160 --> 00:49:05.980
but just presenting another
opportunity to revisit this.

00:49:05.980 --> 00:49:07.080
So the question is,

00:49:07.080 --> 00:49:08.050
I think the appetite

00:49:08.050 --> 00:49:11.030
for doing more development in
Afghanistan is constrained,

00:49:11.030 --> 00:49:13.170
not by the view that we
failed in Afghanistan,

00:49:13.170 --> 00:49:15.060
but by the argument that
offering development aid

00:49:15.060 --> 00:49:17.580
will legitimize the Taliban government

00:49:17.580 --> 00:49:19.263
or prop them up in some way.

00:49:20.230 --> 00:49:22.940
She goes on to say development
aid is never neutral,

00:49:22.940 --> 00:49:25.380
so how do we do it
ethically in this context

00:49:25.380 --> 00:49:26.980
without being seen to,

00:49:26.980 --> 00:49:30.123
or actually propping up
the Taliban government?

00:49:31.040 --> 00:49:32.010
Rory?

00:49:32.010 --> 00:49:33.827
- Well, there's no easy answers to this,

00:49:33.827 --> 00:49:36.130
and we need to be realistic about this.

00:49:36.130 --> 00:49:38.000
I mean, we can produce spin.

00:49:38.000 --> 00:49:39.960
And obviously the principal needs to be,

00:49:39.960 --> 00:49:41.290
to try to make sure that

00:49:41.290 --> 00:49:42.636
the development and humanitarian aid

00:49:42.636 --> 00:49:44.810
reaches ordinary Afghans

00:49:44.810 --> 00:49:46.450
and doesn't reach the Taliban government.

00:49:46.450 --> 00:49:47.630
But as I've tried to explain,

00:49:47.630 --> 00:49:49.100
nothing is ever quite that simple

00:49:49.100 --> 00:49:52.527
because if an NGO fills up
their car at a gas station,

00:49:52.527 --> 00:49:54.927
they will pay sales tax on that.

00:49:54.927 --> 00:49:56.560
And some of that will go
to the Taliban government.

00:49:56.560 --> 00:49:59.840
And that is unfortunately true,

00:49:59.840 --> 00:50:01.120
not just in Afghanistan.

00:50:01.120 --> 00:50:04.030
It's true if you're doing
humanitarian aid in South Sudan,

00:50:04.030 --> 00:50:05.720
it's true if you're doing
humanitarian development aid

00:50:05.720 --> 00:50:07.140
almost anywhere around the world.

00:50:07.140 --> 00:50:10.113
You have to make difficult
compromises with the government.

00:50:11.000 --> 00:50:13.500
The leverage though that
we should be exercising

00:50:13.500 --> 00:50:14.680
of the Taliban government,

00:50:14.680 --> 00:50:16.793
is around diplomatic recognition.

00:50:17.830 --> 00:50:19.747
And maybe you could say to them,

00:50:19.747 --> 00:50:21.430
"Look, we're going to provide

00:50:22.670 --> 00:50:25.470
a generous level of humanitarian
and development support

00:50:25.470 --> 00:50:26.340
to the Afghan people,

00:50:26.340 --> 00:50:27.880
but if you want more than that,

00:50:27.880 --> 00:50:29.230
if you want diplomatic recognition,

00:50:29.230 --> 00:50:30.850
if you want more of these opportunities,

00:50:30.850 --> 00:50:33.050
these are the things you have to do."

00:50:33.050 --> 00:50:34.241
But don't use,

00:50:34.241 --> 00:50:35.660
as I keep saying,

00:50:35.660 --> 00:50:38.330
starving people as a way

00:50:38.330 --> 00:50:40.570
of trying to threaten
the Taliban government,

00:50:40.570 --> 00:50:42.070
it's not going to work.

00:50:42.070 --> 00:50:43.300
They're not going to pay any attention.

00:50:43.300 --> 00:50:45.380
It hasn't worked anywhere in the world.

00:50:45.380 --> 00:50:47.240
It's a very, very mad thing to do.

00:50:47.240 --> 00:50:48.073
And remember,

00:50:49.310 --> 00:50:51.823
above all, what that will
mean is if you do this,

00:50:53.240 --> 00:50:58.240
if the Taliban government
finally goes in 20 years' time,

00:50:58.420 --> 00:51:00.691
Afghans will be very, very rightly angry

00:51:00.691 --> 00:51:02.868
with the international community.

00:51:02.868 --> 00:51:04.390
If you want to have a good
relationship with Afghanistan,

00:51:04.390 --> 00:51:05.440
you're not going to achieve it

00:51:05.440 --> 00:51:07.883
by starving the people and
pretending that by doing so,

00:51:07.883 --> 00:51:11.040
you're going to change the
behavior of the Taliban.

00:51:11.040 --> 00:51:11.873
- Thanks, Rory.

00:51:11.873 --> 00:51:12.750
And building on that question,

00:51:12.750 --> 00:51:14.230
there's another question here

00:51:14.230 --> 00:51:17.370
that I'd love for Khalid
to jump in and answer.

00:51:17.370 --> 00:51:18.203
And the question is,

00:51:18.203 --> 00:51:19.440
are there practical ways the West

00:51:19.440 --> 00:51:20.863
can engage with the Taliban

00:51:20.863 --> 00:51:23.710
without invoking feelings of
negotiating with terrorists?

00:51:23.710 --> 00:51:27.380
So kind of building on what
Rory just responded to,

00:51:27.380 --> 00:51:29.090
but Khalid I think you could respond

00:51:29.090 --> 00:51:30.140
to this specifically.

00:51:30.997 --> 00:51:35.453
- I completely second
Rory on this part as well.

00:51:36.334 --> 00:51:37.167
But as he said,

00:51:37.167 --> 00:51:39.940
there are no easy straightforward answers.

00:51:39.940 --> 00:51:41.280
But what's the alternative?

00:51:41.280 --> 00:51:43.970
The alternative of shutting them off

00:51:43.970 --> 00:51:45.710
and doing no engagement.

00:51:45.710 --> 00:51:48.990
You don't use any of the
leverages that you have, you know.

00:51:48.990 --> 00:51:51.420
So then you also play into the hands

00:51:51.420 --> 00:51:53.560
of the hardliners within Taliban.

00:51:53.560 --> 00:51:54.900
And they would say, you know,

00:51:54.900 --> 00:51:57.440
these Westerners, these infidels,

00:51:57.440 --> 00:51:58.970
you know, they are never straight.

00:51:58.970 --> 00:52:00.980
And look what they've done.

00:52:00.980 --> 00:52:03.930
You are providing a breeding ground

00:52:03.930 --> 00:52:06.410
for future generations of terrorists,

00:52:06.410 --> 00:52:08.570
or even people who are angry.

00:52:08.570 --> 00:52:11.570
Angry at the West of being betrayed

00:52:11.570 --> 00:52:13.690
and left alone.

00:52:13.690 --> 00:52:18.600
So I think engagement on humanitarian plus

00:52:20.840 --> 00:52:24.030
should be a gateway for
further discussions.

00:52:24.030 --> 00:52:27.790
I think they've shown some
willingness on discussions

00:52:27.790 --> 00:52:30.610
on girls' education, for example,

00:52:30.610 --> 00:52:35.610
which is a welcome
improvement and development,

00:52:37.160 --> 00:52:38.840
but more should be done.

00:52:38.840 --> 00:52:39.673
But still,

00:52:41.050 --> 00:52:43.490
I believe if there is the issue

00:52:43.490 --> 00:52:46.930
of going to the Taliban government

00:52:46.930 --> 00:52:49.610
and then financing going
to them as a concern,

00:52:49.610 --> 00:52:51.350
there are ways, as I
said earlier you know,

00:52:51.350 --> 00:52:53.780
you have national programs, for example,

00:52:53.780 --> 00:52:54.730
the citizen charter

00:52:54.730 --> 00:52:58.570
or national basic package
of health services.

00:52:58.570 --> 00:53:03.180
You just have to replace
the government agency

00:53:03.180 --> 00:53:06.960
that used to do the
oversight for another agency.

00:53:06.960 --> 00:53:07.990
Well, probably UN.

00:53:07.990 --> 00:53:09.573
UN is engaging through them.

00:53:11.150 --> 00:53:13.470
So that's a start.

00:53:13.470 --> 00:53:18.370
And Taliban would want
sustainability of their rule.

00:53:18.370 --> 00:53:22.990
A very angry population is
not very conducive for that.

00:53:22.990 --> 00:53:25.270
So they understand that.

00:53:25.270 --> 00:53:27.958
But then there is a limit to all of this.

00:53:27.958 --> 00:53:31.887
At what cost are we
going to isolate Taliban?

00:53:31.887 --> 00:53:36.720
At the cost of 30 million
people starving to death?

00:53:36.720 --> 00:53:39.610
I think it's not going to look good

00:53:39.610 --> 00:53:44.610
on the whole Western idea
of freedom and human rights.

00:53:45.896 --> 00:53:49.900
So you have to engage with them.

00:53:49.900 --> 00:53:53.810
And as Rory and Orzala have mentioned,

00:53:53.810 --> 00:53:56.700
we don't have to go far
to see for an example

00:53:56.700 --> 00:53:59.280
of how isolation has not worked, you know.

00:53:59.280 --> 00:54:02.420
Look at our neighboring
Iran in the last 40 years.

00:54:02.420 --> 00:54:04.590
Crippling sanctions, you know,

00:54:04.590 --> 00:54:06.096
for people who've been there, you know,

00:54:06.096 --> 00:54:08.480
hyperinflation, but what has happened?

00:54:08.480 --> 00:54:13.330
They've increased their
grasp on the system

00:54:13.330 --> 00:54:15.220
and population more.

00:54:15.220 --> 00:54:18.920
I think abandoning
discussions with the Taliban

00:54:18.920 --> 00:54:20.520
might not be the right approach.

00:54:21.477 --> 00:54:22.470
- One other question

00:54:22.470 --> 00:54:24.310
I'd love to just squeeze
in from the audience

00:54:24.310 --> 00:54:26.147
before coming to each of
you with final thoughts.

00:54:26.147 --> 00:54:29.100
We've been talking about
a lot about the West

00:54:29.100 --> 00:54:31.130
and interactions with
Afghanistan moving forward.

00:54:31.130 --> 00:54:33.730
And there's a question
about regional politics,

00:54:33.730 --> 00:54:35.660
neighboring countries and larger powers

00:54:35.660 --> 00:54:36.800
like China and Russia.

00:54:36.800 --> 00:54:38.810
So I wonder if anyone
would want to jump in

00:54:38.810 --> 00:54:39.883
on that question.

00:54:43.440 --> 00:54:46.510
- Maybe I could add a couple of points

00:54:46.510 --> 00:54:50.830
before Rory and Orzala have their views.

00:54:50.830 --> 00:54:55.830
I think you have to study
every neighbor, you know.

00:54:57.310 --> 00:54:58.753
Pakistan has warned us.

00:54:58.753 --> 00:55:01.090
It's something that we
haven't recognized enough

00:55:01.090 --> 00:55:05.200
is this is a Pakistan-enabled
invasion, you know.

00:55:05.200 --> 00:55:07.690
Regardless of what issues
we had in the government,

00:55:07.690 --> 00:55:09.860
we had an elected government,

00:55:09.860 --> 00:55:13.068
a democracy that was improving.

00:55:13.068 --> 00:55:17.053
It's better than what's
happening right now, you know.

00:55:19.510 --> 00:55:22.480
They did not want an Indian
influence and they've got it.

00:55:22.480 --> 00:55:24.160
I don't think they want a stable,

00:55:24.160 --> 00:55:25.760
fully stable Afghanistan either.

00:55:25.760 --> 00:55:27.820
The status quo is the ideal situation

00:55:27.820 --> 00:55:32.390
for ISI and Pakistan to have
in Afghanistan, you know,

00:55:34.500 --> 00:55:36.510
as long as it's contained.

00:55:36.510 --> 00:55:40.020
People who have a longer
view in Pakistan are worried,

00:55:40.020 --> 00:55:43.300
and they're worried that this fanaticism

00:55:43.300 --> 00:55:45.913
will spill over the
borders and it already has.

00:55:48.380 --> 00:55:52.480
Russia, it was a difficult situation.

00:55:52.480 --> 00:55:54.390
They wanted US out of the region,

00:55:54.390 --> 00:55:58.537
but they also don't want the extremism

00:55:58.537 --> 00:56:03.527
crossing the Amu River into
the Central Asian countries.

00:56:05.660 --> 00:56:08.090
With China, you know,

00:56:08.090 --> 00:56:12.600
I believe as long as it's
contained in Afghanistan,

00:56:12.600 --> 00:56:14.470
they have access to the minerals

00:56:14.470 --> 00:56:18.320
and the lithium and copper and all that.

00:56:18.320 --> 00:56:20.410
That's a win for them

00:56:20.410 --> 00:56:24.750
and for Iran as well, you know.

00:56:24.750 --> 00:56:29.750
I think they wanted US
losing in Afghanistan,

00:56:32.046 --> 00:56:35.400
but they want stable right now.

00:56:35.400 --> 00:56:36.810
They want stable borders

00:56:36.810 --> 00:56:40.250
so they could dump their low quality fuel

00:56:40.250 --> 00:56:43.130
and other products that
they cannot do anywhere else

00:56:43.130 --> 00:56:44.700
because of the sanctions.

00:56:44.700 --> 00:56:46.630
With Uzbekistan and and others,

00:56:46.630 --> 00:56:49.870
as long as they have borders and exports

00:56:49.870 --> 00:56:51.520
and it's contained in Afghanistan,

00:56:51.520 --> 00:56:52.620
that's fine, you know.

00:56:54.010 --> 00:56:55.250
And there's somebody,

00:56:55.250 --> 00:56:57.230
not a lot of them mind
the current situation,

00:56:57.230 --> 00:56:58.910
as long as it stays in Afghanistan

00:56:58.910 --> 00:57:01.150
and does not create bigger headaches.

00:57:01.150 --> 00:57:04.130
But that view might be a short-term view.

00:57:04.130 --> 00:57:08.420
The long-term unstable
Afghanistan at the heart of Asia

00:57:08.420 --> 00:57:12.093
is not good for the whole
region, but also the whole world.

00:57:13.280 --> 00:57:14.167
- Thank you Khalid.

00:57:14.167 --> 00:57:16.882
Rory, did you want to
jump in on that quickly?

00:57:16.882 --> 00:57:18.533
- No no, I'll leave you,

00:57:18.533 --> 00:57:19.940
'cause you're heading
towards the end aren't you?

00:57:19.940 --> 00:57:22.080
You want to do some final comments?

00:57:22.080 --> 00:57:23.330
- Yeah, if you don't mind.

00:57:23.330 --> 00:57:24.680
We do just have a couple minutes left.

00:57:24.680 --> 00:57:27.100
So maybe anything you want to
note on that, we can get to.

00:57:27.100 --> 00:57:29.810
But I just want to, as we close,

00:57:29.810 --> 00:57:31.120
we've covered so much ground

00:57:31.120 --> 00:57:32.370
and again,

00:57:32.370 --> 00:57:35.659
our task today was certainly
to look at how we got here,

00:57:35.659 --> 00:57:37.830
the state of the country currently,

00:57:37.830 --> 00:57:40.937
but particularly Afghanistan's future.

00:57:40.937 --> 00:57:42.350
And so I wonder,

00:57:42.350 --> 00:57:44.470
just in our last couple minutes here,

00:57:44.470 --> 00:57:46.330
and I hate to ask such a big question

00:57:46.330 --> 00:57:48.300
with only about 30 seconds
left for each of you.

00:57:48.300 --> 00:57:51.910
But you know, you've all
made so many great points.

00:57:51.910 --> 00:57:54.492
What's one thing you want
to leave our audience with

00:57:54.492 --> 00:57:56.910
in terms of Afghanistan's future.

00:57:56.910 --> 00:57:57.820
A call to action,

00:57:57.820 --> 00:58:00.290
specifically for the international
development community

00:58:00.290 --> 00:58:02.560
which we've discussed quite a bit.

00:58:02.560 --> 00:58:05.070
Maybe we'll start with you Orzala,

00:58:05.070 --> 00:58:06.340
and then we'll hear from Khalid

00:58:06.340 --> 00:58:07.640
and we'll close with Rory.

00:58:09.170 --> 00:58:10.329
- Thank you, Catherine.

00:58:10.329 --> 00:58:14.200
I hope to be able to say it in 30 seconds.

00:58:14.200 --> 00:58:19.200
But my personal message to the
international development aid

00:58:19.680 --> 00:58:22.960
and to those who are
willing to support a sponsor

00:58:22.960 --> 00:58:26.670
is to invest on the
Afghanistan's young generation,

00:58:26.670 --> 00:58:30.220
provide them with scholarship
opportunities now,

00:58:30.220 --> 00:58:32.090
before it becomes too late,

00:58:32.090 --> 00:58:34.010
particularly girls.

00:58:34.010 --> 00:58:36.280
I am working on a personal mission on this

00:58:36.280 --> 00:58:39.190
together with another Yale world fellow,

00:58:39.190 --> 00:58:41.700
as we are at Yale
university I will say this,

00:58:41.700 --> 00:58:42.533
Lorna Solis,

00:58:42.533 --> 00:58:44.490
she's also a world's fellow

00:58:44.490 --> 00:58:49.150
and I'm hoping to get your
support for this mission.

00:58:49.150 --> 00:58:51.840
Starting with a small
group of young Afghan girls

00:58:51.840 --> 00:58:53.930
and expanding it to a larger one.

00:58:53.930 --> 00:58:57.000
The best way forward in the longer term

00:58:57.000 --> 00:58:59.850
is to invest on young
generation scholarships

00:58:59.850 --> 00:59:01.750
across different themes and subjects.

00:59:01.750 --> 00:59:04.440
Not only social or political sciences,

00:59:04.440 --> 00:59:08.130
but very critical subjects
for the future of the country.

00:59:08.130 --> 00:59:09.110
Thank you.

00:59:09.110 --> 00:59:10.420
- Thank you. Khalid?

00:59:12.750 --> 00:59:15.270
- Yeah, I think act now

00:59:15.270 --> 00:59:17.720
before six to nine months,

00:59:17.720 --> 00:59:19.890
or even three to six months of wait,

00:59:19.890 --> 00:59:21.173
which might be too late.

00:59:23.570 --> 00:59:25.370
But, for my fellow Afghans,

00:59:25.370 --> 00:59:28.200
you know, somebody who's seen it,

00:59:28.200 --> 00:59:30.453
somebody who's been
part of this government

00:59:30.453 --> 00:59:31.286
that has collapsed,

00:59:31.286 --> 00:59:35.580
somebody who shares part
of the blame, you know,

00:59:35.580 --> 00:59:39.130
I am a big critic of myself as well.

00:59:39.130 --> 00:59:42.623
I think all hope is not lost.

00:59:43.940 --> 00:59:48.940
We should use this to examine
the things that went wrong.

00:59:51.430 --> 00:59:54.440
If we have a better
opportunity in the future,

00:59:54.440 --> 00:59:56.910
what sort of democracy would be built.

00:59:56.910 --> 00:59:57.960
Something that's built

00:59:57.960 --> 01:00:01.150
in the matter of the West and copies it?

01:00:01.150 --> 01:00:04.563
Or is there some modifications
that we would want to do?

01:00:05.520 --> 01:00:09.490
I think the international
community on development aid

01:00:09.490 --> 01:00:11.490
should also have lessons, you know.

01:00:11.490 --> 01:00:16.375
There were so many
nitty-gritty details on aid

01:00:16.375 --> 01:00:20.370
that could have made better
impact in the lives of people.

01:00:20.370 --> 01:00:22.570
I hope those lessons are learned

01:00:22.570 --> 01:00:24.250
and when we intervene in Afghanistan,

01:00:24.250 --> 01:00:26.340
we re-intervene, or somewhere else,

01:00:26.340 --> 01:00:31.333
we make better use of the
tax dollars that are paid.

01:00:33.791 --> 01:00:34.717
Thank you. Rory?

01:00:34.717 --> 01:00:36.030
- Well I think my final thing is,

01:00:36.030 --> 01:00:37.990
be generous, be quick, have hope

01:00:37.990 --> 01:00:39.750
and remember that when
you're talking about hope,

01:00:39.750 --> 01:00:42.080
you're having hope in Afghans themselves.

01:00:42.080 --> 01:00:43.803
And I think Dr. Orzala and Khalid

01:00:43.803 --> 01:00:45.630
have been an extraordinary illustration

01:00:45.630 --> 01:00:46.627
of why the international community

01:00:46.627 --> 01:00:49.030
should be hopeful about Afghanistan.

01:00:49.030 --> 01:00:52.560
We've had two individuals
speaking whose adult lives

01:00:52.560 --> 01:00:55.090
really have been dominated
by the last 20 years.

01:00:55.090 --> 01:00:56.640
And what you're seeing

01:00:56.640 --> 01:01:00.223
in what you just witnessed is remember,

01:01:00.223 --> 01:01:02.063
both Dr. Orzala and Khalid

01:01:02.063 --> 01:01:03.910
have been through incredible trauma.

01:01:03.910 --> 01:01:06.420
Their family, their friends,

01:01:06.420 --> 01:01:08.570
their entire lives have
been turned upside down.

01:01:08.570 --> 01:01:10.440
And yet you can see,

01:01:10.440 --> 01:01:12.360
they are talking with incredible grace,

01:01:12.360 --> 01:01:15.690
humility, thoughtfulness, objectivity.

01:01:15.690 --> 01:01:17.670
They're not putting their
own self-interest forward,

01:01:17.670 --> 01:01:21.080
they're trying to frame
constructive positive solutions

01:01:21.080 --> 01:01:23.510
in an unbelievably difficult situation.

01:01:23.510 --> 01:01:25.650
And so that should really give us hope

01:01:25.650 --> 01:01:26.810
because that is of course

01:01:26.810 --> 01:01:28.820
what people like me
love about Afghanistan,

01:01:28.820 --> 01:01:30.420
that's why the international community

01:01:30.420 --> 01:01:33.170
should believe in the
future of Afghanistan.

01:01:33.170 --> 01:01:34.003
- I love that.

01:01:34.003 --> 01:01:35.300
If we were all together in person

01:01:35.300 --> 01:01:36.850
I know the audience would be applauding,

01:01:36.850 --> 01:01:37.870
and I would as well.

01:01:37.870 --> 01:01:39.770
So thank you so much to our panelists

01:01:39.770 --> 01:01:43.110
for this conversation
on Afghanistan's future.

01:01:43.110 --> 01:01:45.200
Again, this conversation
was part of a larger series,

01:01:45.200 --> 01:01:46.440
the Yale Development Dialogues,

01:01:46.440 --> 01:01:48.620
which we'll be continuing, so stay tuned.

01:01:48.620 --> 01:01:50.620
And thanks so much to our
audience for tuning in.

01:01:50.620 --> 01:01:51.820
We'll see you next time.

